            {
                id host-owns-signals
                title "Signal delivery reads only the atomic sink; the disposition half (Host trait, §5.4) transfers to public-api"
                priority 0
                kind @refactor
                deps (no-ambient-state)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T15:02:35.382314651Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @blocker
                        text "**Obligation transferred from `move-state`: the trap table and `vforked` cannot become fields until `onsig` can answer one question without a receiver. `move-state` completed with its scope narrowed; this is the third of three transfers, and the question was never guessed at.**\n\n**The question is narrower than a survey first suggested**, which matters because the answer is probably cheap. `trap.rs:280 onsig` reads the trap table in exactly two places and **both are presence tests, not reads of the action**:\n\n```\nif signo == SIGCHLD { gotsigchld = 1; if trap[SIGCHLD].is_none() { return; } }\n...\nif signo == SIGINT && trap[SIGINT].is_none() { intpending = 1; }\n```\n\nEverything else `onsig` touches is already excluded by `docs/api-design.md` §5.1 and relocated by §5.3 — `gotsig`, `pending_sig`, `gotsigchld`, `error::intpending`. So `sh.traps` is blocked by **one bit per signal**: *is a trap set for N?* — which the handler must answer without a `&mut Shell` and without allocating, and which today it answers by indexing a `static mut [Option<BString>; NSIG]`.\n\n> Does `SignalSink` grow a second atomic array — \"a trap is set for signal N\" — mirroring `sh.traps`, written at every site that assigns or clears `trap[n]`, and read by the handler in place of the two `is_none()` tests?\n\n**Two things make it a real question rather than a formality.**\n\n1. **Write ordering.** The mirror must never say \"no trap\" while the table says there is one, or a `SIGINT` arriving in that window sets `intpending` and the shell takes the interrupt instead of running the user's trap — a visible behaviour change, and **exactly the kind the differential corpus cannot see, because it sends no signals**. The assignment sites are `trapcmd`, the fork reset that clears traps in a child, and `dotrap`'s own bookkeeping; `INTOFF`/`INTON` brackets are the existing tool.\n2. **Whether the mirror is the shell's or the sink's.** It is derived from `sh.traps`, so two shells in one process would need two mirrors — but the handler is installed per-*process* and gets no argument saying which shell it is for. That is the same limit §6 documents for the locale and `getopt`, and it should be recorded there as a further entry rather than discovered later. §5.3's `Arc` makes the sink shareable; it does not make it per-shell.\n\n**`vforked` is the same shape and travels with it.** `jobs.rs:229 vforked` is not in §5's table, and `onsig` reads it too. A signal handler has no receiver, so anything it reads stays a `static mut` until the sink grows a place for it. (`onsigchild` looks like a third instance and is not — it is `#[cfg(any())]`, dash's never-satisfied `#ifdef SYSV`, never compiled.)\n\n**Nothing else in the trap group is blocked:** `sigmode` and `trapcnt` are read only from ordinary frames, so once this is answered the table moves as a unit with them. And `move-state` confirmed that neither `output.rs`'s `SHELL_IO` nor `input.rs`'s stack is read by `onsig`, so this blocker is confined to `trap.rs` and `vforked`.\n\n**Coverage note for whoever takes it:** the port has ten interactive `^C` pty cases (`tests/harness/ptydiff.py`), which are the only automated coverage that delivers a real signal. They pass today. Any mirror added here should be exercised by extending that set, because the 61,592-case differential corpus structurally cannot reach it — the same rule `move-state` recorded for the line editor: when a change touches a path the corpus cannot execute, the corpus being green is not evidence about it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T15:51:53.499601001Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "**The `onsig` presence-bit question, answered: yes, the sink grows the mirror — and three of the question's own premises are wrong. Recorded before any code, per the node's own discipline.**\n\nThe question transferred from `move-state` was: *does `SignalSink` grow a second atomic array — \"a trap is set for signal N\" — mirroring `sh.traps`, written at every site that assigns or clears `trap[n]`, and read by the handler in place of the two `is_none()` tests?* The answer is **yes**, and the three corrections below are what makes it implementable rather than a formality.\n\n**Correction 1 — the handler asks about two signals, not N.** `onsig` reads the table at exactly two indices, `trap.rs:287` (`SIGCHLD`) and `:295` (`SIGINT`), and re-reading them confirms `move-state`'s sharpening: both are presence tests, never reads of the action. The array is still declared `[AtomicBool; NSIG]`, because the writers index by `signo` and uniformity costs 65 bytes and no branch — but the *behavioural* surface is two bits, and the tests that pin it are per-signal rather than generic.\n\n**Correction 2 — `INTOFF`/`INTON` is not the tool, and cannot be.** The question nominates the existing brackets for the write ordering. They do not do this job. `INTOFF` increments `suppressint`, which defers *delivery of an interrupt*; it does not stop `onsig` from running, and since `errors-are-values` step F `INTON` is not a delivery point at all (`docs/divergences.md`'s `error.interrupt-delivery-point`, and `error.rs:102-106` where the `onint(sh)` is gone). A handler entered inside an `INTOFF` bracket reads the mirror exactly as it would outside one. The bracket makes a mutation atomic against *taking* an interrupt, never against *observing* the table.\n\n**Correction 3 — there is no safe one-sided ordering, because the two signals want opposite ones.** The question states the hazard as one-directional: \"the mirror must never say no-trap while the table says there is one\". Measured against the readers, both directions are observable, and in opposite senses:\n\n| | mirror says trapped, table says none | mirror says none, table says trapped |\n|---|---|---|\n| **SIGINT** | `intpending` is not set; `dotrap`'s loop clears `gotsig`, finds `trap[SIGINT] == None` and `continue`s (`trap.rs:359-372`) → **the ^C is swallowed**, and the four `EINTR` poll sites retry their syscall instead of raising, which is the \"shell stops answering ^C\" failure mode | `intpending` is set → **the shell takes the interrupt instead of running the user's trap** (the question's hazard) |\n| **SIGCHLD** | `gotsig[SIGCHLD-1]` and `pending_sig = SIGCHLD` are set → `wait` answers `128 + pending_sig` = **145** (`bltin/wait.rs:51,:89`) and `waitproc`'s `sigsuspend` loop leaves early (`jobs.rs:1429-1433`) | the CHLD trap **misses one delivery** |\n\nSo \"update the mirror on the side that is safe for the interleaving\" has no answer: each signal's safe side is the other's unsafe side. The pair must be **atomic against delivery**, and the tool the codebase already uses for exactly that is `trap::sigblockall` / `system::sigclearmask` — `jobs.rs:1908-1910`'s `xtcsetpgrp` is the precedent and exists for the same reason. Blocking also restores the property the C has for free and the mirror destroys: in dash `trap[signo]` is one pointer, so the handler reads either the old value or the new one and never an inconsistent pair.\n\n**Where the brackets go, and what they cost.** `trap[n]` is assigned in exactly three places: `builtins/trap.rs:100` (`trapcmd`), `trap.rs:111,:117` (`clear_traps`, the fork reset), and `trap.rs:432` (`exitshell`, slot 0 — `EXIT` is not a signal number and `onsig` can never read it, so it needs nothing). Both real sites already bracket with `INTOFF`/`INTON` at the right granularity, so the mask bracket is **hoisted to the same region**: one pair per `trap` command, one per fork — not one per slot, which in `clear_traps`'s NSIG loop would be 130 syscalls per fork. To keep the discipline from being a convention, `TrapTable::set` takes a `&SignalsBlocked` witness — an RAII guard that blocks on construction and restores the saved mask on drop — so a slot cannot be written outside the bracket and still compile. (`clear_traps` does not run in a vforked child: `forkchild` gates `forkreset` on `lvforked == 0` at `jobs.rs:963`, so the guard's `Drop` is never on the no-destructor path.)\n\n**Whose mirror — and the answer dissolves the sub-question.** The sink's. And it creates **no new limit**, because §5.3's inbox is *already* per-process: `gotsig`, `pending_sig`, `gotsigchld` and `intpending` are read and written by a handler that is installed per-process and receives only `signo`. The mirror is exactly as per-shell as the rest of the inbox, which is to say not at all. `Arc` makes the sink shareable, not per-shell — `move-state` said this and it is right. What is actually owed is that **`docs/api-design.md` §6 does not list the signal inbox** among the process-wide facts, beside the locale, `strtok` and `getopt`. That is a further §6 entry and it is this node's to write. The mirror is *derived from* `sh.traps` and *stored in* the sink, so `TrapTable` holds the handle and `TrapTable::set` is the single writer of both.\n\n**`vforked` is not shell state and must not become a field.** This corrects `move-state`'s transfer note, which says it \"is the same shape and travels with it\". It travels to the *sink*, not to `Shell`. `jobs.rs:1139` sets `vforked = mypid` in the **parent, before `vfork`**, and `:1159` clears it in the parent after; the child reads it out of the shared address space to learn that it is the child (`getpid() != vforked`, `trap.rs:281`). That is a property of an address space, not of a shell — a `sh.vforked` would be the parent's field written by the parent and read by a process that only accidentally shares it. It becomes an `AtomicI32` in the sink. It also needs **no** mask bracket, and the reason is worth keeping: the handler's test is `vforked != 0 && getpid() != vforked`, which is self-checking — in either window the parent reads `getpid() == vforked` and proceeds correctly.\n\n**What this unblocks.** With `onsig` reading only the sink, nothing asynchronous touches `trap.rs`'s tables, and `trap`/`ptrap`/`trapcnt`/`sigmode` move onto `Shell` as `sh.traps: TrapTable` in one commit — the whole of `move-state`'s group-A signal row, plus `vforked`. Nothing else in the crate is blocked on it: `sigmode` and `trapcnt` are read only from ordinary frames.\n\n**Storage, and why this does not wait for `public-api`.** The sink lands as a `static SINK` of atomics — which is *not* a `static mut`, so the declarations it absorbs are removed from the count rather than moved. `public-api` later gives it ownership (`Arc`, `Host::attach`, a `Shell` field); this node lands the storage and the discipline. That order is forced: `Host::attach` needs `Shell` to own a `Box<dyn Host>`, which needs §2's `Builder`, which is `public-api`'s.\n\n**Scope finding, and it is the node's title.** \"The frontend claims signal dispositions\" has two halves and only one of them can land here. The **delivery** half is the sink, above. The **disposition** half is §5.4's `Host` trait, and it transfers to `public-api` for the same reason `io`+`streams` did — it cannot exist before the builder. The seam is already measured and small: seven `trap::setsignal` call sites (`trap.rs:81,:113,:407-409`, `builtins/trap.rs:102`, `jobs.rs:473-475,:991-992,:1016-1018`), two `ignoresig` (`jobs.rs:994-995`), the two `sigaction` calls inside `setsignal` itself (`trap.rs:193` query, `:232` install), and `redir.rs:466-470`'s five raw `libc::signal` calls in the here-document child. Policy stays the library's — `setsignal` decides *which* disposition, including the `mflag`/SIGTSTP exception at `trap.rs:174-178` — and mechanism becomes the host's, which is what §5.4 already says.\n\n**One finding outside the question, recorded because it changes another node's inventory.** `error.rs:61 suppressint` is listed by §5.1 as handler state. It is not, any more: since step F `onsig` does not read it (`trap.rs:296-306` says so in as many words). It is touched only from ordinary frames and is therefore ordinary shell state that could become a field — `errors-are-values`' or `public-api`'s to take, not this node's, but it should stop being counted as signal state.\n\n**Coverage, which had to come first and did.** Seventeen pty cases landed before any of this, green on both shells. Ten of them exist specifically for the table above: each of the four mirror directions has the case that catches it going the wrong way, `trap ignore INT then ^C` separates `is_some()` from `is_empty()` as the predicate, and `wait status no CHLD trap` is the control that makes `wait status w/ CHLD trap` mean something. The other seven are job control proper — suspend/resume, the terminal changing hands, SIGTTIN, and the reaping a shell only reports at a prompt — none of which the corpus can execute."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:13:32.27441512Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "**Two POSIX `kill` cases fail deterministically at `b064da8` and were not on the pre-existing list. Found from `[[owned-words]]`; recorded here because the evidence points at this node's ground, not because I have diagnosed it.**\n\nRunning the POSIX suite for the pattern-matcher slice, HEAD scored `cases: PASS=658 FAIL=53` against a logged `660/51`. The two extras are **`sig-kill-option-s`** and **`sig-kill-xsi-signal-forms`**.\n\nThey are not `[[owned-words]]`'s. The failing *set* is byte-identical at `9768aae`, at `5f2c091`, and at **`b064da8`** — which is `docs: Record the signal-sink decision and its three corrections`, this node's own commit and the start of `owned-words`. So they were present before that node touched anything.\n\n**They are also not the documented flaky fringe**, and this is the part worth acting on. The plan's list of pre-existing POSIX failures names `exit-unrecoverable-read-error-stdin` / `-dot` and `suspend-shell-restores-its-own-terminal-settings`; these two are not on it. Their behaviour:\n\n* fail in **every** full-suite run (four runs, identical sets — not flaky in the usual sense),\n* pass **5/5** when run in isolation via `run.py --case sig-kill-option-s --case sig-kill-xsi-signal-forms`,\n* pass **5/5** when the case script is piped by hand to either the port or reference dash.\n\nSo the failure is deterministic *given the full suite* and absent otherwise — an ordering or contention effect between cases, not a property of the shell in isolation. Both cases are trap-plus-signal-delivery scripts:\n\n```\ntrap 'printf \"upper\\n\"' ALRM\nkill -s ALRM $$ || exit 1\ntrap 'printf \"lower\\n\"' HUP\nkill -s hup $$ || exit 1\nkill -s 0 $$ || exit 1\n```\n\nand both fail by losing the **second** trap's output (`upper,signal-zero`, missing `lower`), which is the shape of a disposition or pending-signal state surviving from an earlier case rather than a name-parsing bug — `kill -s hup` by itself is fine.\n\nTwo things follow, and they are different jobs. The **harness** gap is real and already named in the plan: `tests/harness` re-runs a divergent corpus case eleven times before calling it flaky and `run.py` has no equivalent, so anything order-dependent surfaces as a phantom regression. The **shell** question — whether a trap disposition or a pending signal is leaking across a case boundary, and whether that is the port's or is shared with reference dash — is this node's, and I have not answered it. Reference dash was only tested on the isolated script, where both agree; nobody has run the *reference* through the full suite to see whether it loses `lower` too. That one run is the next step and it decides which of the two jobs this is.\n\nLogs: `posix.log`, `posix2.log` (HEAD), `posix-5f2c091.log`, `posix-b064da8.log`, with the sorted failing sets beside them, in this session's scratchpad."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:15:31.262123626Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "**Correction to the note above, from the one run it said was the next step: I ran it, and the answer removes the harness's excuse.**\n\n(Filed as a note, not errata — errata attaches to work already done, and this node's work is not.)\n\nThe previous note left it open whether the two `sig-kill` failures were a harness ordering artifact or a port defect, and said the deciding experiment was to put *reference dash* through the full suite. That was one command and I should have run it before filing, rather than filing a question I could answer in three minutes.\n\n`python3 posix/harness/run.py --shell tests/.build/ref/src/dash` — full suite, `SKIP=0`, so every case ran: `cases: PASS=653 FAIL=58`, and **`sig-kill-option-s` and `sig-kill-xsi-signal-forms` are not in its failing set.** Reference dash passes both in exactly the run order that makes the port fail them.\n\nSo this is **a port defect, not a harness classification gap.** The order dependence is real, but it is the port that is order-dependent: something an earlier case leaves behind changes how a later `kill -s <name>` plus `trap` behaves, and the C shell in the same sequence is unaffected. The failure shape from the previous note stands and now means more — the *second* trap's output is the one lost (`upper`, then `signal-zero`, with `lower` missing), while `kill -s hup` in isolation is fine on both. That points at trap-disposition or pending-signal state surviving a case boundary in the port, which is this node's ground.\n\nTwo corrections to what I wrote, then:\n\n* \"an ordering or contention effect, not a property of the shell in isolation\" — wrong as stated. It is a property of *this* shell, exposed by ordering. Reference dash under identical ordering does not have it.\n* \"the harness gap is real and already named\" — still true and still worth fixing, but it is no longer an explanation for these two. A re-run-and-classify step in `run.py` would have marked these FLAKY and *hidden a genuine port defect*, which is the same failure mode `divergences.sh`'s header warns about for the register: a classification that is too permissive is worse than none.\n\nNote also that the port scores better overall than the reference (`658/53` against `653/58`), so the port being ahead in aggregate is exactly why a two-case regression has to be tracked by *set* and not by count — the aggregate would have absorbed it silently.\n\nStill not diagnosed, and still not `[[owned-words]]`'s: the failing set is byte-identical at `9768aae`, `5f2c091` and `b064da8`. Logs `posix-refdash.log` alongside the others in the `owned-words` session scratchpad."
                    }

