                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:24:17.128116376Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "**The two `sig-kill` cases are not a port defect. Reference dash fails them identically once the comparison is controlled for load, and the two notes above are wrong in the direction that matters.**\n\n(A note rather than errata, for the reason the previous note gives: errata attaches to work already done, and this node's is not — the plan lint enforces it.)\n\nThe note at 23:13 asked whether this was a harness artifact or a port defect. The note at 23:15 answered \"port defect\" from one reference run that passed both cases. I re-ran that comparison with the one variable it left free — **system load** — held equal, and the answer reverses.\n\n**The controlled experiment.** Forty spinners up (load average 25–50, which is this machine's ordinary state: other agents run `clang++`, `rustc` and a mutation sweep continuously), then the full POSIX suite four times, alternating shells:\n\n```\nport round 1   cases: PASS=658 FAIL=53   sig-kill-option-s + sig-kill-xsi-signal-forms BOTH FAIL\nref  round 1   cases: PASS=653 FAIL=58   BOTH FAIL\nport round 2   cases: PASS=658 FAIL=53   BOTH FAIL\nref  round 2   cases: PASS=653 FAIL=58   BOTH FAIL\n```\n\nThe reference's stdout is **byte-identical to the port's** in both cases — `'upper\\nsignal-zero\\n'` for `option-s`, `'by-number\\ndone\\n'` for `xsi`. Not a similar failure: the same one.\n\n**And the same port passes them when the machine is quiet.** A full run at low load scored `cases: PASS=660 FAIL=51` — the 51-name core exactly, zero missing, zero extra. Diffing the loaded set against the quiet set gives exactly two names, and they are these two. The port's own result moves with the load and with nothing else.\n\n**Neither `-s` nor `-SIGNAL` parsing is implicated, and the earlier reading of the failure shape was wrong.** The prior note says both cases \"fail by losing the second trap's output\". True of `option-s`, false of `xsi`, where the lost output is `by-name` — the *first*. The invariant is not position, it is the signal: **both cases lose precisely the SIGHUP trap and nothing else.** In the very same scripts `kill -s ALRM $$` fires, `kill -14 $$` fires, `kill -s 0 $$` returns 0. A defect in the argument grammar cannot pass `-s ALRM` and `-14` in one script while dropping `-s hup` and `-HUP` in another.\n\n**Isolation, re-measured, agrees with the earlier note and goes further.** `run.py --case` on the two: 5/5 pass on the port under load, 5/5 without. And the raw harness invocation — the exact `sandbox --unshare all … --new-session -- <shell> -c <script>` the executor builds — run 200 times in a loop under load: **0/200 mismatched on the port, 0/200 on the reference.**\n\n**\"Ordering\" was never the mechanism, and this is what the earlier notes could not have known.** I bisected the run order. `--case` preserves `CASES` order, so the exact full-run prefix through the target — 663 cases, identical sequence — is a faithful replay of everything that precedes it. That prefix is **clean**, at moderate load and again at load ~24. Nothing an earlier case leaves behind is doing this: replaying every earlier case in order does not reproduce it.\n\n**Every instrument perturbs it, including ones that cannot.** A forking probe (`grep /proc/self/status`) before the traps: passes. A fork-free probe (`while read … < /proc/self/status`, builtins only) in the same place: passes. A probe appended *after the last command*, adding nothing before the `kill`: passes. When it passes the shell's own words are unremarkable — `SigCgt` has SIGHUP set, `SigIgn` is SIGPIPE alone, `SigBlk` and `SigPnd` zero, the trap table lists both traps, `$$` is 2.\n\n**The conclusions to strike, precisely.**\n\n* \"It is a property of *this* shell, exposed by ordering. Reference dash under identical ordering does not have it.\" — The ordering *was* identical; the load was not. Under identical load the reference has it too.\n* \"a port defect, not a harness classification gap\" — reversed. The re-run-and-classify step that note argued against is the thing that would have classified these correctly. The argument was sound in general and wrong here: it was defending a defect that turns out not to exist.\n* \"both fail by losing the second trap's output\" — the shared factor is SIGHUP, not ordinal position.\n\n**What I did not do, and why.** I did not change `kill`. There is nothing in `builtins/kill.rs` to fix: I read the grammar against `killcmd` and `decode_signal`/`decode_signum` and both are faithful to `jobs.c`, and the experiment says the shell is not the variable. Manufacturing a fix for a defect the reference shares would have been worse than no fix, and would have been the third wrong conclusion on the same two cases.\n\n**What I did instead.** Pinned the class in the differential corpus, where a real regression *would* be caught and where load cannot manufacture one: **21 new cases in `tests/corpus/curated_signals.txt`** covering `-s NAME` upper/lower/mixed, `-s NUMBER`, `-NAME`, `-NUMBER`, `-s 0`/`-0`, both failing POSIX scripts as differentials, the `SIG` prefix dash rejects, unknown names, missing operands, `kill -l` by status, an ignored trap and a reset one. `PASS=23 FAIL=0` on both shells. Mutation-checked rather than assumed: reducing the `-s` branch to `decode_signum` (numbers only) turns **9 of the 23** red.\n\n**One thing I could not explain and will not pretend to.** Why a case at position 663 fails in a 711-case run and passes in a 663-case run of the identical prefix at the same load. Causality says the later cases cannot reach it, so the carrier is something outside the case sequence — the harness process's own accumulated state is the obvious suspect. Both shells share it, so it is not this node's ground. The actionable form: **these two names are load-sensitive in `posix/harness` and belong on the known-fringe list beside `exit-unrecoverable-read-error-*` and `suspend-shell-restores-its-own-terminal-settings`, not in the 51-name core.** Track POSIX by set, as the earlier note rightly said — but read a set difference against the load the run was taken under.\n\nLogs in this session's scratchpad: `load-port-{1,2}.log`, `load-ref-{1,2}.log` with sorted sets, `full2.log` (the quiet 660/51), `posix-hos1.log` (the first loaded 658/53), `probe-load.log`, `tailprobe.log`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:24:44.591281878Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "**The mask bracket is hoisted, as the decision says — but the decision's stated reason for hoisting is false, and the real reason is a different one. Recorded because the code arrived per-write and I moved it back.**\n\nThe salvaged tree implemented `SignalsBlocked` per *write* rather than per region, with this reason in its commit message: `clear_traps` `continue`s past every slot without a non-empty action, so a shell with no traps — nearly every fork — blocks signals zero times, and hoisting would cost one `sigprocmask` pair on every fork instead. **That measurement is correct and it falsifies the decision's own argument.** The decision says hoisting avoids \"130 syscalls per fork\" in `clear_traps`'s NSIG loop; the `continue` means a per-slot guard was never going to cost 130, it was going to cost zero in the common case and two per *trapped* signal otherwise. The syscall count does not decide this.\n\nThe decision's conclusion still stands, on a reason it did not give. **`clear_traps` does not merely write slots — in the `simplecmd` arm it clears a slot, calls `setsignal`, and puts the action back.** Per-write guards make each of those two stores atomic and leave the shell *observably untrapped across the pair*: between them the slot is `None` and the bit is false, and a `SIGINT` landing there sets `intpending` and is taken instead of running a trap the shell is about to have again. That is the same class of window the mirror was introduced to close, reopened one level up. Hoisting closes it, and the price is one pair per fork against a fork's own ~100µs — which is the trade the decision reached by the wrong route.\n\nTwo measured corrections to where the decision put the brackets:\n\n* **`trapcmd`'s `INTOFF`/`INTON` is per *word*, not per command.** The decision says \"both real sites already bracket with `INTOFF`/`INTON` at the right granularity, so the mask bracket is hoisted to the same region: one pair per `trap` command, one per fork\". Those are two different regions in `trapcmd`: its `INTOFF`/`INTON` sits *inside* `for word in signals`, so \"the same region as INTOFF\" would have been one pair per signal named. I followed the explicit parenthetical — **one pair per `trap` command** — so `trap 'act' INT TERM HUP` blocks once, not three times. The guard is created above that loop and outside the `INTOFF` pair.\n* **`clear_traps` is one pair per call**, which is one per fork, matching both readings.\n\n`exitshell`'s slot-0 `EXIT` write still takes nothing, via a separate `take_exit_action`; `EXIT` is not a signal number and `onsig` can never read it. That part of the decision was right as written.\n\nTwo documents already described the hoisted form and the code did not: `docs/api-design.md` §5.3 (\"the bracket is hoisted to the two writers, `trapcmd` and `clear_traps` — one pair per `trap` command, one per fork\") and `SignalsBlocked::new`'s own doc comment (\"Hoist it: … not one per slot\"). Moving the code to the design removed both false statements rather than rewriting two documents to match an undocumented late change. The doc comment keeps the instruction and loses the false cost claim, which is now the region argument above."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T11:53:31.193890807Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "**Final gates, on `8a48ec8` (binary `709c0728`, built from the rebased tree in this worktree's own target dir). Green; the six commits are landed on master by fast-forward.**\n\n```\ncargo test -p nsh   231 = the rebased master's 227 + this node's 4\nlib warnings        69  (was 73 before owned-words' expansion slices)\nptydiff             PASS=60 FAIL=0 FLAKY=0  -- all 60, including the\n                    17 job-control/presence-bit cases and all ten ^C cases\nrunall              PASS=61699 FAIL=0 FLAKY=31 XFAIL=4, all 116 corpora,\n                    tests/.build/fail holds 14 .flaky + 4 .xfail and zero .out\nPOSIX               cases PASS=660 FAIL=51 -- the 51-name core EXACTLY,\n                    byte-identical to the earlier quiet run's set,\n                    zero missing, zero extra\nunsafe operations   3075 -> 3044  (-31)\nstatic mut decls    49 -> 44      (-5)\n```\n\n**Both trend figures were re-derived by the method rather than inherited.** Unsafe ops: build with `allow(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)` removed and subtract the ordinary warning count — 3148−73 at `f9dc0ec` in a throwaway worktree, 3117−73 here. That reproduces the 3075 baseline exactly, which is the check that the method was applied the same way twice. `static mut` by `docs/errors-are-values.md`'s documented command, 49 at the node's base against 44 now. The five that left are `trap`, `ptrap`, `trapcnt`, `sigmode` and `jobs.rs`'s `vforked`; the sink that absorbed them is a plain `static` of atomics, so they were removed from the count rather than relocated within it.\n\n**The POSIX run is the third independent confirmation of the sig-kill finding.** At load 4.4 the suite scores the core exactly and both `sig-kill-option-s` and `sig-kill-xsi-signal-forms` pass; the only `sig-kill` name in the failing set is the pre-existing `sig-kill-job-id-operand`. Under load both fail, on the port and on reference dash alike. Quiet run, loaded run, loaded reference run — the same conclusion three ways.\n\n**Two operational notes for whoever runs these next.**\n\n* **`ptydiff` could not run in this worktree at all until its build directory stopped being a symlink.** `tests/.build` was symlinked to the root checkout's, and `sandbox` refuses to create a bind mountpoint through a symlink on a read-only root — every case died with `Can't mkdir parents for …`, `PASS=0 FAIL=60`, which reads exactly like a catastrophic regression and is not one. Reproduced in one command outside the harness: the same bind succeeds against the real path and fails against the symlinked one. Fixed by giving this worktree a real `tests/.build` with only `ref` symlinked back, which also removes the shared-run-root race that made `ptydiff` need to be exclusive.\n* **The corpus sweep's tally shares a line with its progress output.** `runall.sh` writes corpus names to stderr with `\\r` and no newline, so `==== TOTAL … ====` lands after the last name on that same line and `grep '^===='` finds nothing. The sweep had in fact completed — 116 of 116 corpora named, `FAIL=0`, no `.out` — and was very nearly re-run for an hour on the strength of a grep. Match on `TOTAL`, not on a line start."
                    }

