                    {
                        id delete-libc-calls
                        title "The 155 libc calls that std, core or bstr already do, per docs/libc-surface.md"
                        priority 0
                        kind @refactor
                        deps (delete-memalloc)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-09T18:56:01.001624416Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "This node exists because 394 direct `libc::` call sites had no owner, and `libc::putenv` surviving into a crate that has `std::env` is what exposed it — surviving while holding a pointer into shell-owned storage that glibc does not copy, which is a live use-after-free.\n\n`docs/std-replacements.md` asks which *reimplementations* std already does: `mystring.rs`, `output.rs`'s `vsnprintf`, the hash tables. It lists direct libc calls as *riders* — \"what to delete while you are already in there\". That framing is the whole reason nothing got done: a rider is only done if someone is already in that file for another purpose, and no node's title sends anyone to `histedit.rs` or `bltin/test.rs`. Eight sites in each, and both files are untouched by every node in the plan.\n\n`docs/libc-surface.md` is the measurement, symbol by symbol. 100 distinct symbols, 394 sites, four classes:\n\n* **A, 116 sites in 19 symbols** — `<[u8]>`/`bstr`/`core::ptr` one-liners. `strlen` 39, `strchr` 17, `strcmp` 15, `strcpy` 7, `strspn` 5, `memmove` 5, `memcpy` 5, `strpbrk` 4, and eleven more. The compiler decides these, but only once the operand is an owned value — converting the call while it is still `*mut c_char` buys a cast, not a deletion. That is why this node deps on [[delete-memalloc]] rather than running beside it.\n* **B, 39 sites in 12 symbols** — std has it and one local argument settles it: the last raw `free`/`malloc`/`realloc`, `putenv`, `getopt`, `getcwd`, `chdir`, `abort`, `snprintf`, and the stdio four that ride [[output-is-a-writer]].\n* **C, 18 sites in 8 symbols** — traps. `isalpha`/`isalnum`/`isspace`/`isdigit`, `strcoll`, `atoi`, `strtod`, `fnmatch`, `strerror`, `strsignal`, the three `stat64` variants. std has each and the behaviour differs; `docs/std-replacements.md` §5 measures why. None may be swapped without either showing the difference unobservable or registering it.\n* **D, 221 sites in 61 symbols** — std does not have it, and these stay. `fork`, `vfork`, `execve`, `waitpid`, the nine signal calls, `tcsetpgrp`, `sigprocmask`, `setpgid`, `getrlimit`, `memfd_create`. Recording the class *is* the deliverable: a reader who does not find them listed as deliberately kept will spend an afternoon proposing `std::process::Command`.\n\nSo the honest headline is that most of this crate's libc surface is correct — it is a POSIX shell and a shell that stopped calling `fork` would not be one. 155 of 394 are not.\n\nScheduling: class A is not a rider, it is the second half of [dec:nsh:owned-data] seen from the call site rather than the allocation, and it goes per file alongside the container work rather than after it. Class B's `putenv` is a defect, not a cleanup, and does not wait for this node."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-12T08:43:23.424730657Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-12T10:14:46.834486194Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Closed. Classes A and B of `docs/libc-surface.md` are at zero except four sites, and §7 of that document records the outcome symbol by symbol.\n\nOf the 155 sites the node was scoped to, 47 had already gone as consequences of the nodes it waited on — [[delete-memalloc]] took the `free`/`malloc`/`realloc` and the `strspn`/`strdup`/`bsearch` group, [[output-is-a-writer]] took the stdio four and `snprintf`, [[owned-vars]] took `putenv` (the use-after-free in §5, which is fixed), and the builtin restructure took `getcwd` and `strtok`. This node converted the remaining 104 in seven commits, one per concern: comparisons, byte scans, block moves, lengths (twice, to stay inside the WIP budget), errno, and `abort`/`chdir`.\n\n**Four sites remain and all four are outside the node's title**, which is what std, core or bstr already do:\n\n* `input.rs` `set_errno` and `mystring.rs` `atomax` write errno. std reports the last OS error and cannot assign one.\n* `shellmain.rs` `main` wants errno's *address*, which std does not expose at all. It is `main.c`'s cache of the TLS lookup and is ported for the pointer.\n* `builtins/fc.rs` `scan_options` calls `getopt(3)`. std has no `getopt`, so this is a rewrite and not a replacement — and what is wrong with it is that `optind` is a process global and the scan permutes `fc`'s array, which is [[no-ambient-state]]'s question. The note already in that file said as much before this node started, and it was right.\n\nThree conversions are deliberately not the obvious one, each documented where it stands: `pmatch` keeps a byte-by-byte `strncmp` (`mystring::ncmp_eq`) because the C relies on the early stop at a shared NUL to read past a single stack byte only as far as the terminator; `argstr`'s `strcspn` and `pmatch`'s two scans count to the first stop byte rather than take `CStr`'s whole remaining string, because both re-enter per control byte; and three `strchr` sites keep the terminator *in* the set, because `strchr` matches it — that is how `read` escapes a NUL from the input, how a NUL counts as an IFS separator, and what `umask` does at the end of a mode.\n\n**Gates.** `runall.sh 6` PASS=61514 FAIL=0 FLAKY=40 XFAIL=4, no stale register entries; divtest PASS=44 FAIL=0; ptydiff PASS=37 FAIL=0; POSIX cases PASS=660 FAIL=51. Every one is the baseline exactly. `cargo test -p nsh` 160 + 11, green.\n\n**Unsafe ops: 3829 → 3779**, and the small size of that number is the honest result rather than a disappointment. `CStr::from_ptr` is itself an unsafe call, so converting a `strlen` on a `*const c_char` trades one unsafe call for another; what the metric moves on is the errno consolidation, where `system::errno` is safe and four `unsafe fn` wrappers and their dereferences went with it. `docs/libc-surface.md` §2 predicted this — the call site is downstream of [dec:nsh:owned-data], and converting it while the operand is still a raw pointer buys a cast rather than a deletion. The rest of the drop is [[move-state]]'s and whatever follows it.\n\nAlso recorded: a review of this node's diff widened to its neighbours and found seven defects in [[printf-builtin]], two reproduced, one of which panics the shell. Filed as errata there rather than fixed here, because this node's oracle is that nothing changes."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-12T10:14:51.965438121Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id owned-words
                        title "Word expansion and parsing operate on owned bytes, not C pointers"
                        kind @refactor
                        deps (move-state)
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-15T16:22:31.806222247Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-15T16:50:01.727781937Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Baseline and conversion order, recorded before touching code, on wt/owned-words off b064da8.\n\n**Baseline, with the command beside it (per errors-are-values 0.6's rule).** `bash measure_unsafe.sh <root>`: comment out `#![allow(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)]` in lib.rs, `cargo check --lib`, take the lib's `generated N warnings` and subtract the ordinary lib warning count. **3345 - 74 = 3271 unsafe ops**, which reproduces the figure the operator directive recorded at 17fc5c6 exactly. By class: 2393 calls-to-unsafe-fn, 609 raw-pointer derefs, 219 mutable-static uses, 16 union accesses. By file, the top two are this node's: expand.rs 639, parser.rs 303. `unsafe fn` declarations, `grep -rhcE '^\\s*(pub(\\([a-z]+\\))? )?(const )?unsafe fn '`: **512**.\n\nOne hazard for whoever re-measures: cargo decides freshness by mtime and *replays cached diagnostics* for a fresh crate, so a script that restores lib.rs from a backup gets the previous run's total back. The restore must be followed by `touch`. I got 3345 as the \"ordinary\" count once before noticing.\n\n**What the survey found that changes the plan's framing.** The storage is already owned — `strlist.text` is a `BString` (expand.rs:146), `Rt1::out` is a `BString`, `expbuf`/`globbuf` are `BString`s, `wordtext` is a `BString` on the shell. `delete-memalloc` did that. What is left is the **cursor layer** over that storage: `expdest()` hands out `*mut c_char` into a `BString`'s buffer, the word travels between functions as a `*mut c_char` return, and the NUL terminator is the length. So this node is not \"make the data owned\" — it is \"stop handing out pointers into data that is already owned\", and the deref class is what that kills.\n\n**Order, cheapest-and-most-independent first. Recorded now so the sequence is checkable against what actually lands.**\n\n1. **The expansion buffer's escape hatch.** `expansion_result()` (expand.rs:484) is the only way expand.rs's buffer leaves the file as a bare pointer, and it has exactly two readers — `redir.rs:441` (openhere) and `parser.rs:2348` (expandstr) — both of which `CStr`-ify it immediately. Returning `&BStr` fixes both, and pulls `expandstr`/`getprompt`/`setprompt` and linedit's prompt reader with it. Independent of every other cluster.\n2. **`IfsCache`** (expand.rs:327-342). `ncifs: *const c_char` is a raw pointer *into a shell variable's text*, refreshed only by the `varfunc_t` hook on IFS (var.rs:296) — the same shape as the `putenv` use-after-free `owned-vars` fixed. Four fields, one producer (`changeifs`), five reader sites, no cross-file coupling.\n3. **`*const Syntax` -> an enum** (parser.rs:329 plus nine pointer-identity comparisons). Mechanical and independent.\n4. **The parser's spare-capacity layer.** `getmbc`/`getmbc_at`/`dollarsq_escape` reserve MBSLOP and write through a raw cursor into a `BString`'s spare capacity, then commit with `set_len`; five commit sites, two reserve sites. `escape::conv_escape` is the same shape.\n5. **The expbuf cursor layer** (expand.rs:396-486, eight functions in one 80-line region), index-based.\n6. **`strlist::textp`'s thirteen call sites** -> `&BStr`. Nine are clean; the two hard ones are eval.rs:1215 (the execve argv) and expand.rs:3085 (`strcoll`).\n\n**Deliberately last, and probably not this stretch.** `argstr`/`evalvar`/`subevalvar`/`exptilde`/`expari`/`expbackq` plus `_rmescapes` and `pmatch` are one indivisible unit: `subevalvar`'s `scan` is a function-pointer type over seven `*mut c_char` (expand.rs:1452-1460), so the whole scan family converts together or not at all, and `pmatch` reproduces a deliberate out-of-bounds read (expand.rs:3243-3248) that a slice type would have to be told about. `expmeta` is likewise whole-or-nothing because it recurses through the static glob buffer.\n\n**Process, per the operator's directive.** Per slice: `cargo check` + `cargo test -p nsh`, plus targeted differentials against ref dash, which are seconds. ONE full runall at the end of the stretch, never mid-stretch and never re-run once clean. ptydiff only if the getprompt/setprompt seam is touched — slice 1 touches it, so ptydiff is owed for that one."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-15T21:27:20.881943501Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "**Why `pmatch` was read and deliberately not converted, and what the next agent needs to decide before touching it.**\n\n`pmatch` is the single largest concentration of unsafe operations left in this node — 68 ops, plus `ccmatch` 13, `mbnext` 6, `mesclen`/`esclen` 2 — and by the shape of the rest of the file it looks like the obvious next slice: a pure function over two C strings, three call sites, no shell state. It is not a mechanical conversion, and the reason is worth recording so nobody spends an afternoon rediscovering it.\n\n**The blocker is one line: `expand.rs`, in the `C_LBRACKET` arm.**\n\n```\nlet mut mbs: *const c_char = &c as *const c_char;\n...\n} else if crate::mystring::ncmp_eq(mbs, q, mb as usize) {\n```\n\n`mbs` starts as the address of the *local* `c` — a single byte on this frame — and `mb` is the length in bytes of the character under the string cursor. When the string character is multibyte and the pattern character is not `CTLMBCHAR`, `mbs` stays pointing at that one byte and `ncmp_eq` is asked for up to `mb` of them. The port already knows this: the site carries a `NOTE (bug-for-bug)` saying it reads past the end and is reproduced, and `mystring::ncmp_eq` carries a matching note explaining that it is a hand-written loop rather than a slice compare *specifically* so the read stops at the shared NUL instead of being an unconditional `from_raw_parts(.., n)`.\n\nSo the C's answer in that case is a function of whatever byte follows `c` on the stack. That is not a behaviour a slice type can reproduce, and it is not a behaviour worth reproducing.\n\n**What has to be decided, not transcribed.** `strncmp`'s early stop means the second byte is only ever reached when `c == q[0] && c != 0`. `c` is a plain (non-`CTLMBCHAR`) pattern byte and `q[0]` is the lead byte of a multibyte character, so equality requires the pattern byte to be >= 0x80 — reachable in a non-UTF-8 locale, or when the pattern's high byte was not framed as multibyte. The three candidate resolutions are: (a) treat \"single pattern byte vs multibyte string character\" as never equal, (b) compare only the first byte and call it a match, (c) prove the case unreachable. Each is defensible and they are not equivalent; picking one is a **decision that belongs on `plan/decisions/owned-data.md`**, in the same register as `is_type_unbiased`'s documented refusal to reproduce its out-of-bounds index — which is the precedent, and which chose to make the window real rather than reproduce garbage.\n\n**Why not at the end of this stretch.** The oracle for that decision is the differential corpus — `aud_parser_mb`, `aud_parser_ubmb`, `gap_nonutf8`, `aud_foundation_utf8` — and the protocol allows one full sweep per stretch, spent. A change whose whole risk lives in the cases only the corpus can reach must not be the last thing landed before the sweep budget is gone. It wants its own stretch, starting with a corpus run to establish what the current binary actually does in case (b) territory, then the decision, then the conversion.\n\n**What is mechanical about it, for whoever picks it up.** Everything except that one line. The cursors are ordinary forward walks that become indices; the recursion `pmatch(p, q)` becomes an index pair or a pair of suffix slices; `p.offset(-1)` appears three times and always after an advance; `*p.offset(1)` at the `C_MINUS` test is guarded by the preceding `*p == C_MINUS`, so it cannot read past the terminator. The pattern and string both carry counted terminators already, so the slices exist. Budget the work as one day for the conversion and one decision record, not as a refactor.\n\n**Also relevant and cheaper**: `patmatch`/`pmatch` can be split into a thin `unsafe` adapter that builds the two slices and a safe inner matcher, which concentrates the remaining unsafe at one boundary even before the `mbs` question is settled. That is a legitimate half-step and it does not require the decision — but it does require the corpus, for the same reason."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-15T21:27:53.212699912Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "**First stretch: three slices landed on master, fully gated. Trend and what remains.**\n\nLanded by fast-forward after rebase onto `001fa6e` (master had moved; the only conflict was a one-line timestamp in this node's own claim record in `.state.styx`, resolved to the worktree's).\n\n* `a7c3424` — the expansion buffer stops leaving `expand.rs` as a `char *`. `expansion_result` hands back bytes; `expandstr`/`getprompt` return owned bytes and the prompt seam follows. New shared helper `mystring::cstr_prefix`, safe, which this node will keep using.\n* `482f27c` — `IFS` is owned. `IfsCache::ncifs` was a pointer into a shell variable's text; `ifs_state.ifs` was a pointer carrying exactly one bit and is deleted; `ifsbreakup_slow` takes `&mut` instead of `*mut`.\n* `02bf791` — the encoder appends instead of writing through a raw cursor into spare capacity; the `set_len` commit window is gone. Syntax tables become `SyntaxRef`, a bounds-checked slice accessor, and then `const`s.\n\n**Two defects fixed rather than transcribed**, both on the same pattern — a pointer into shell-variable text held across code that can reallocate it, which is `[[owned-vars]]`'s `putenv` bug seen twice more. `expandstr`'s failure path returned a seed pointer that its own expansion could invalidate (`PS1='$(PS1=x; echo)'` reaches it); `IfsCache::ncifs` was that borrow made permanent. Neither was reachable by the corpus, which is worth noting: this class does not show up as a differential failure, it shows up as a crash under a workload nobody ran.\n\n**Trend, by the documented method (comment out `#![allow(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)]`, `cargo check --lib`, subtract the ordinary lib warning count).**\n\n| | ops | calls | derefs | statics | unions |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| start (`b064da8`) | 3271 | 2393 | 609 | 219 | 16 |\n| after `a7c3424` | 3268 | 2391 | 608 | 219 | 16 |\n| after `482f27c` | 3243 | 2389 | 586 | 218 | 16 |\n| after `02bf791` | 3209 | 2364 | 577 | 218 | 16 |\n\nexpand.rs 639 → 580 ops and 72 → 67 `unsafe fn`; parser.rs untouched at 303. Ordinary lib warnings 74 → 73 (a dead null check deleted), so anyone re-deriving must subtract 73, not 74.\n\n**A measurement hazard, recorded because it cost me a wrong number once.** Cargo decides freshness by mtime and *replays cached diagnostics* for a fresh crate. A script that toggles `lib.rs` and restores it from a backup gets the previous run's warning total back, because the restored file's mtime is older than the artifact. Every toggle must be followed by `touch`. The first figure I took this way was 3345 reported as the \"ordinary\" count.\n\n**Gates for the stretch.** `runall.sh 6`: PASS=61592 FAIL=0 FLAKY=32 XFAIL=4, no STALE, and all fifteen files in the fail directory are `.flaky` or `.xfail` — zero `.out`. Exact baseline. divtest 44/0. ptydiff 43/0, run once for the slice that touched the getprompt/setprompt seam and deliberately skipped for the other two, which are nowhere near the line-editor read path. `cargo test -p nsh` 226/0 and 73 warnings on every commit. POSIX suite skipped for the whole stretch: nothing here changes conformance-visible behaviour that the corpus does not already cover — the three slices are a return type, a cache's ownership, and a write strategy, and all three are byte-identical by construction. Targeted differentials carried the real load: 22 here-document/PS4 cases, 40 IFS/field-splitting cases, and 42 encoder cases run under three locales for 126 comparisons, each byte-identical including exit status, and each spot-checked to confirm it was exercising the arm it names rather than agreeing on an error.\n\n**What remains, in the order I would take it.** The `pmatch` decision is written up in its own entry above and is the largest single item. Beyond it: `expmeta` (60 ops) is whole-or-nothing because it recurses through the static glob buffer, and the glob buffer's cursor layer (`globstnputs`, `addfnamealt`, `expmeta_rmescapes`, ~28 ops) can only follow it, because `expmeta`'s frames recover their prefix length by appending at a cursor *below* the buffer's end — an append-only conversion changes that and has to be argued, not assumed. `_rmescapes` (39) has three destinations and wants doing with `subevalvar`. The `argstr`/`evalvar`/`subevalvar`/`exptilde`/`expari`/`expbackq` core (~145) is still one unit for the reason the first entry records: `subevalvar`'s `scan` is a function-pointer type over seven `*mut c_char`, so the scan family converts together or not at all. `parser.rs` is untouched and its 303 ops are flat and mostly *not* this node's — the top entries are `checkkwd`/`quoteflag` statics and `pgetc` calls, which belong to `statics-that-are-not-state` and the input layer; the genuinely word-shaped part is `getmbc`/`getmbc_at`/`dollarsq_escape` writing through a raw cursor into a `BString`'s spare capacity (~15), which is the same conversion `02bf791` just did for the encoder and should be cheap."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-15T21:41:07.114006934Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "**Correction: two `unsafe fn` figures in my own commit messages are wrong, and both are wrong the same way.** Commit messages are immutable, so the correction lives here. (Filed as a note rather than errata because errata attaches to work already done — the refusal is recorded on [[printf-errata]] and it is right.)\n\n* `02bf791` says \"expand.rs 614 -> 580 and its `unsafe fn` count **72 -> 67**\". The op counts are right. The `unsafe fn` count was **71 -> 67**, a drop of four, not five.\n* `67d47de` says \"parser.rs 299 -> 286 and its `unsafe fn` count **41 -> 40**\". The op counts are right. The `unsafe fn` count was **40 -> 40 — no change at all**. `getmbc` stayed `unsafe fn` because it still calls `mbrtowc` and `pgetc_eoa`; nothing in that commit dropped a declaration.\n\n**The mistake is identical in both and it is not a typo.** I ran the counting command, got the *after* value, and wrote a *before* value from memory instead of running the same command against the parent commit. The measurement was real; the baseline was invented to make it a delta. That is the failure `docs/errors-are-values.md` §0.5 already caught twice in this project — a figure recorded without its method beside it — and §0.6's rule \"a metric is the command, not the number\" covers this case exactly: **a delta needs the command run twice.**\n\n**Re-derived with both ends measured by the same command**, `grep -rhcE '^\\s*(pub(\\([a-z]+\\))? )?(const )?unsafe fn ' <path>`, over the node so far (`b064da8` -> `ab0776e`):\n\n| | start | now |\n|---|---|---|\n| crate-wide `unsafe fn` | 512 | 508 |\n| expand.rs | 71 | 67 |\n| parser.rs | 40 | 40 |\n\nSo every `unsafe fn` this node has retired is in expand.rs, and they are the four the encoder slice took: `chtodest` became a plain `fn`, and `BASESYNTAX`/`SQSYNTAX`/`is_type_unbiased` stopped being `unsafe fn`s when they became `const`s.\n\n**That is the honest shape of it: the cascade has barely begun to collapse.** A declaration only drops when a body stops performing *every* unsafe operation, and the bodies in these two files still reach `mbrtowc`, `pgetc`, the mutable statics, and each other. The unsafe-*op* count is the leading indicator and it has moved well (3271 -> 3160, derefs 609 -> 573); the `unsafe fn` count is the lagging one the operator directive actually cares about, since its endpoint is \"nsh denies `unsafe_code`\". 508 is the real distance left.\n\n**Two consequences for whoever reports next.** Report both ends of every figure, measured, in the same hour. And expect the `unsafe fn` count to stay nearly flat through the rest of *this* node: it will not move much until the five statics leave expand.rs and the syscall floor leaves for its own crate, which are `no-ambient-state`'s and `unsafe-is-a-crate`'s work. A future stretch that reports a large `unsafe fn` drop without those having happened should be disbelieved and re-measured."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-15T22:27:47.519673095Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "**The second batch's sweep, adopted and re-run — and why the inherited one did not count.**\n\nI was handed a runall from the previous session to adopt. There was no `runall` process left on the box; its log (`…/9ff8c4bd…/scratchpad/runall2.log`, finished 23:59) read `PASS=61592 FAIL=0 FLAKY=47 XFAIL=4`, exact baseline. I nearly recorded it as the validation of `7e6065b`/`67d47de` and did not, because of the timestamps: `target/debug/nsh` was built at **23:35:18** and `67d47de` — the getmbc/memalloc slice, 122 lines of `parser.rs` — was committed at **23:36:29**, with every touched source file's mtime later than the binary's. The sweep was green against a binary that predated the second commit's source. A tally is only evidence about the binary it ran, and that binary was not HEAD.\n\nRebuilt at `5f2c091` and re-ran. The old binary hashed `ad8c8918…`, the HEAD one `d43ed09a…` — they were genuinely different artefacts, so this was not a paranoid re-run.\n\n**Fresh sweep, `runall.sh 8` at `5f2c091`: PASS=61592 FAIL=0 FLAKY=41 XFAIL=4, zero STALE, and the fail directory holds 15 `.flaky` + 4 `.xfail` and zero `.out`.** Exact baseline. The escape-decoder and getmbc slices are validated. (FLAKY drifts run to run — 32, 47, 41 — because it counts retried races, not defects; FAIL and the `.out` count are the numbers that mean anything.)\n\n**Worth keeping as a rule, because it nearly cost a false green:** a sweep's evidence is `sha256sum` of the pinned binary plus the commit it was built from, not the wall-clock order of \"commit, then sweep\". `runall.sh` already hard-links both shells at start precisely so a mid-run rebuild cannot swap them — that protects against the swap *during* a run and says nothing about what went *into* it. Checksum before, name the commit, or the tally is unattributable."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-15T23:12:38.154339691Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "**Second stretch: `pmatch` converted and the decision taken, plus one small arm. Trend, gates, and a POSIX finding that is not this node's.**\n\nLanded by fast-forward onto master, which did not move under me.\n\n* `f7101c5` — the pattern matcher takes slices. `pmatch` is now a three-operation adapter (two `CStr::from_ptr`, `fnmatch`) over a safe `pmatch_bytes`; `ccmatch` stopped needing a writable pattern and is a plain `fn`; `mbnext` keeps a pointer form for its four other callers as a three-byte window over `mbnext_bytes`. The matcher moved to `crates/nsh/src/pmatch.rs` and the generic slice readers to `mystring.rs`, beside `ncmp_eq`.\n* `9768aae` — `varvalue`'s `$-` arm appends instead of filling a `makestrspace` reservation and committing with `expdest = p`. Small, but it leaves `expmakestrspace` with exactly one caller (`_rmescapes`).\n\n**The `pmatch` decision, which the previous entry asked for.** Resolution (a): a one-byte bracket member is not a multibyte character and does not match one. The predecessor's write-up left three candidates and said the case might be unreachable; it is not. An instrumented build enters the branch with **`c=0xc3 sc=0xc3 mb=4`** — so the C reads *three* bytes past a one-byte local, not one, and `mb` is `ml + 2` rather than the character length I had expected from the prose. The probe is `p=$(printf '\\303'); s=$(printf '\\303\\251'); case $s in [$p])`, whose bytes I checked with `od` rather than assuming: a lone `\\303` is not valid UTF-8, so nothing frames it `CTLMBCHAR`, while `é` in the string is framed.\n\n**No divergence-register entry, deliberately.** The two answers agree wherever the C is defined (`mb == 1` is byte equality; a differing first byte is a miss under both) and can part only where the C's answer was stack contents. Reference dash answers \"no match\" on the probe 40 runs out of 40. An entry must be able to say which side is right about an *observable* difference; there is none, and registering it would add an entry that can never fire — the exact failure `divergences.sh`'s own header warns about. It is written up in `plan/decisions/owned-data.md` instead, against the `IS_TYPE_UNBIASED` precedent in the same file, which refused the same trade for the same reason.\n\n**The function-density gate refused the first attempt, and was right.** Splitting an unsafe monolith into a safe core plus a thin adapter is more functions by construction, so `expand.rs` went 75 → 82. The structural answer was not a bypass but a file: the matcher is a pure function over two byte strings with no shell state and had no business in a 3900-line expansion file. Worth generalising — **this node will keep tripping that gate**, because \"more, smaller, safe functions\" is what it does; the fix each time is to ask what the cluster actually is, not to raise the limit.\n\n**Trend, both ends of every figure measured with the same command in the same hour** (`measure_unsafe.sh`: toggle `#![allow(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)]`, `touch`, `cargo check --lib -p nsh`, subtract the 73 ordinary lib warnings). Two counting bugs found and fixed in the method itself: the class sentence appears on the `note:` lines as well as the `warning[E0133]:` header, which double-counted every class; and ordinary warnings carry `-->` spans, which inflated the per-file counts. Both counts now come from a parse that pairs each E0133 header with the `-->` beneath it. The self-check is that the E0133 count equals the ops delta exactly, and at `5f2c091` it reproduces the recorded 3160 / 580 / 286 figures.\n\n| | ops | calls | derefs | statics | unions |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| node start (`b064da8`) | 3271 | 2393 | 609 | 219 | 16 |\n| stretch start (`5f2c091`) | 3160 | 2347 | 573 | 224 | 16 |\n| after `f7101c5` | 3078 | 2294 | 544 | 224 | 16 |\n| after `9768aae` | 3075 | 2292 | 543 | 224 | 16 |\n\nPer file: expand.rs 580 → 490, the new pmatch.rs 5, mystring.rs 27 unchanged (the moved helpers are safe), parser.rs 286 unchanged. `unsafe fn` at both ends by the same grep: crate **508 → 507**, expand.rs 67 → 64, pmatch.rs 2, parser.rs 40. The one declaration retired is `ccmatch`. That is the predecessor's prediction holding exactly — the cascade does not collapse until the statics and the syscall floor leave, and 507 is the distance.\n\n**Gates.** Full sweep at `9768aae`, binary `282e343a` built from that commit: `runall.sh 8` **PASS=61592 FAIL=0 FLAKY=23 XFAIL=4**, zero STALE, fail directory holding 10 `.flaky` + 4 `.xfail` and zero `.out`. Exact baseline. One sweep this stretch beyond the adoption re-run, as the protocol allows. divtest 44/0. `cargo check` 73 warnings and `cargo test -p nsh` 226/0 on every commit. Targeted differentials: 106 pattern cases covering every arm of the matcher under two locales, and 31 `$-` cases, each byte-identical including exit status and each spot-checked to be exercising the arm it names — `set -euf; echo \"[$-]\"` prints `[ufe]`, not an agreed empty string. Corpora re-run against the slice binary: aud_parser_mb 58, aud_parser_ubmb 16, aud_parser_glob 50, gap_nonutf8 3, aud_foundation_utf8 3, globcheck 6.\n\n**ptydiff skipped**, with the reason: neither slice is anywhere near the line-editor read path. The prompt seam was `a7c3424`'s and is untouched; this stretch is the pattern matcher and one variable-expansion arm.\n\n**POSIX run, not skipped, because pattern matching is conformance-visible** — and it turned up something worth having. The suite scores `cases: PASS=658 FAIL=53` at HEAD. That is two worse than the `660/51` in the older log, and the two extras are `sig-kill-option-s` and `sig-kill-xsi-signal-forms`. They are **not this stretch's and not this node's**, and the evidence is direct rather than inferred: the failing *set* at `9768aae` is byte-identical to the set at `5f2c091`, and byte-identical again to the set at **`b064da8`, this node's own start commit**. Four full runs, sets diffed rather than counts compared, which is the discipline an earlier stretch already established here after `660/51` turned out not to reproduce.\n\nThey are also not the documented flaky fringe: they fail in every run, pass 5/5 in isolation under the harness, and pass 5/5 when the script is run by hand against both shells. So they are deterministic *only in the full-suite run* — an ordering or contention effect, in a suite whose runner has no re-run-and-classify step. Recorded on `[[host-owns-signals]]`, whose node `b064da8` belongs to.\n\n**One process hazard, since it bit twice in one night.** Another session was running `cargo test --workspace` in the main checkout and rebuilt `target/debug/nsh` at 01:09:55, mid-report. The sweep was unaffected because `runall.sh` hard-links both shells at start — but that only protects a run in progress. The lesson from the adoption note stands and is now doubled: name the commit and checksum the binary *before* the run, because on this box `target/debug/nsh` is shared and can change under you between one command and the next."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-15T23:13:11.46945129Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "**What remains, and the `expmeta` question sharpened to the point where it can be answered.**\n\nMeasured, not remembered — the per-function census is `cargo check --lib` with the allow toggled off, each `warning[E0133]:` header paired with the `-->` beneath it and mapped to its enclosing `fn`. At `9768aae`, expand.rs's 490:\n\n| ops | function |\n|---|---|\n| 60 | `expmeta` |\n| 43 | `argstr` |\n| 39 | `_rmescapes` |\n| 32 | `subevalvar` |\n| 27 | `varvalue` |\n| 23 | `expbackq` |\n| 19 | `evalvar` |\n| 17 | `removerecordregions`, `expandmeta_glob` |\n| 15 | `scanright`, `ifsbreakup`, `expandmeta`, `expmeta_rmescapes` |\n\n`parser.rs`'s 286 is still mostly *not* this node's — `command` 57, `readtoken1` 22, `readtoken`/`parsesub` 19 each are `checkkwd`/`quoteflag` statics and `pgetc` calls, which belong to `statics-that-are-not-state` and the input layer.\n\n**`expmeta` is the next slice and it is more tractable than the last entry implies.** That entry said the glob buffer's frames \"recover their prefix length by appending at a cursor *below* the buffer's end\", and that an append-only conversion changes that and has to be argued. Having read it: the argument is already written down in `globstnputs`' own doc comment, and it dissolves the objection rather than blocking it.\n\n`globstnputs(s, n, p)` does `set_len(p - base)` *first*, then appends. So \"append at a cursor below the end\" is exactly **truncate-to-index, then append** — and that is a perfectly ordinary operation on an owned buffer. The conversion is therefore not append-only, it is `truncate` + `extend_from_slice`, and saying it that way removes the hazard instead of arguing about it. Concretely:\n\n* `enddir` becomes an index. `globgrowto` disappears into the append.\n* `globstnputs(dname, len, enddir)` becomes `b.truncate(i); b.extend_from_slice(..); i + len`.\n* `*enddir.offset(-1) = C_SLASH` becomes `b[i - 1] = b'/'` — an indexed store below the counted end, bounds-checked, no cursor needed.\n* `enddir = cp.offset(expdir_len)` after a child returns becomes `b.truncate(expdir_len)` — the frame's rewind, said out loud.\n* The reallocation hazard the C re-reads `stackblock()` for **vanishes**, because indices do not move. Every `cp = globbase()` re-read exists only to survive a growth.\n* `debug_assert_eq!(globb().len(), expdir_len)` at entry becomes trivially true rather than an invariant to maintain.\n\n**The one thing that genuinely has to be decided is `expmeta_rmescapes`, and `addfnamealt`'s comment already states it.** `addfnamealt` says: \"`n` runs past the length when the caller is the no-metacharacter branch, whose `expmeta_rmescapes` wrote through a raw cursor without committing. Those bytes are written; they are only uncounted.\" So `addfnamealt` reads *past* `globb().len()` on purpose, into written-but-uncounted spare capacity, and counts them itself. Convert `expmeta_rmescapes` (15 ops) to append and those bytes become counted — at which point `addfnamealt`'s `n` and the buffer's length say the same thing and one of the two has to stop being computed separately. **That is the question to answer first, before touching `expmeta`'s body:** does `addfnamealt` still need `n`, or does it become `globb()[..]`? It is answerable by reading two functions, and it is the whole of the coupling — the ~28-op cursor layer (`globstnputs`, `addfnamealt`, `expmeta_rmescapes`) should convert *with* `expmeta`, not after it, for exactly this reason.\n\nBudget it as one slice of ~103 ops (`expmeta` 60 + `expmeta_rmescapes` 15 + `expandmeta` 15 + the accessors), gated with `aud_parser_glob` (50 cases), `globcheck` (6) and a targeted corpus for the recursive path — nested directories with metacharacters in more than one component, which is what exercises the truncate-and-rewind. Expect the function-density gate to fire on `expand.rs` again; the answer is the same one it was this stretch.\n\n**After that**, the `argstr`/`evalvar`/`subevalvar`/`exptilde`/`expari`/`expbackq` core (~145) is still one unit because `subevalvar`'s `scan` is a function-pointer type over seven `*mut c_char`, and `_rmescapes` (39) converts with it. `varvalue` (27) is in that cluster too — its `$-` arm is gone but its argv walk and `lookupvar` are not. `removerecordregions` (17) I did not look at and it may be independent; it is the cheapest unexamined item left.\n\n**Resumption state.** Master `9768aae`, tree clean, everything landed by fast-forward. Worktree `…/worktrees/owned-words` on branch `nplan/owned-words`, even with master. Nothing of mine running. `measure_unsafe.sh` — the fixed one, with both counting bugs out — is worth re-creating from the method described in the stretch entry above rather than trusting any number without it."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-15T23:15:45.423899386Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "**Correction to my own stretch entry, two paragraphs up: the POSIX pair is a port defect, not an ordering artifact.**\n\nThat entry ended the POSIX paragraph with \"an ordering or contention effect, in a suite whose runner has no re-run-and-classify step\". I then ran the experiment that sentence implied and had not done — reference dash through the *full* suite — and it passes both `sig-kill-option-s` and `sig-kill-xsi-signal-forms` in the same run order that makes the port fail them (`cases: PASS=653 FAIL=58`, `SKIP=0`).\n\nSo the ordering is real but the defect is the port's, and my sentence gave the harness credit for a bug. The full correction and its consequences are on `[[host-owns-signals]]`. Nothing here changes: the failing set is still byte-identical at `9768aae`, `5f2c091` and `b064da8`, so it remains outside this node — but \"outside this node\" and \"not a real defect\" are different claims and I ran them together.\n\nThe general form, worth keeping: **a differential finding is not explained until the reference has been run through the same path.** I had the reference binary, the harness takes `--shell`, and it cost three minutes."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-16T00:00:31.496589193Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "**Third stretch's slice order, recorded before anything lands, and the `expmeta` coupling answered.**\n\nOrder, taken straight from the per-function census in the resumption note two entries up, cheapest-and-most-coupled first:\n\n1. **`expmeta` and the glob buffer's cursor layer** (`expmeta` 60 + `expmeta_rmescapes` 15 + `expandmeta` 15 + `addfnamealt`/`globstnputs`/`globgrowto`/`globbase`/`globb`). One slice, for the reason the last entry gave: the coupling below has to be resolved with the body, not after it.\n2. `argstr` 43 / `_rmescapes` 39 / `subevalvar` 32 / `varvalue` 27 — the scan family, still one unit because `subevalvar`'s `scan` is a function-pointer type over seven `*mut c_char`.\n3. `parser.rs`'s token buffers, if the runway reaches them.\n\n**The coupling, answered by reading the two functions the last entry named.** The question was: with `expmeta_rmescapes` converted to append, its bytes become counted, so `addfnamealt`'s `n` and the buffer's length become the same number — does `addfnamealt` still need `n`?\n\n**It does not, and the parameter is what goes.** `addfnamealt` has exactly two callers. The readdir-loop one already arrives with `n == globb().len()`, because `globstnputs` commits. The no-metacharacter one is the only place they differed, and they differed *only* because `expmeta_rmescapes` wrote through a raw cursor and left the length alone — which is the thing being removed. So after the conversion both callers agree, `enddir` carries no information the buffer does not, and `addfnamealt(b, expdir_len)` is the whole signature: assert the candidate is a C string, copy it out, truncate to the prefix.\n\nOne consequence that is genuinely new and has no C counterpart, worth stating because it is the only place this conversion *adds* a statement rather than deleting one. In the no-metacharacter branch the C unescapes the tail at the cursor and then asks `lstat`; when `lstat` fails it simply returns, and there is nothing to undo because the bytes were never counted. Counted bytes have to be rewound. So the failure arm gets an explicit `b.truncate(expdir_len)`, and a test names it — `a_rejected_literal_tail_rewinds_the_buffer` in `crates/nsh/tests/glob_buffer.rs`, whose fixture alternates present and absent tails so that every kept candidate follows a rejected one at the same `expdir_len`."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-16T00:18:10.813636834Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "**A gate finding that is bigger than the slice that turned it up: the sweep pins `LC_ALL=C`, so every multibyte path in the shell is differentially untested.**\n\nFiled separately from the slice because it is not about `subevalvar`. `sandboxed.sh` sets `--setenv LC_ALL \"${DS_LOCALE:-C}\"`, deliberately and for a good documented reason (the comment at line 54 records a locale-dependent phantom divergence that cost a worktree). Nothing ever sets `DS_LOCALE`. `runall.sh` does not. So the whole corpus — 114 files at the time of writing — runs under C, and under C:\n\n* `${v%?}` strips one **byte**,\n* `memtodest`/`mbtodest` never emit a `CTLMBCHAR` frame,\n* `pmatch`'s two `CTLMBCHAR` arms are never entered,\n* `scanright`'s rewind over an encoded character never runs,\n* `expmeta`'s `EXP_MBCHAR` encoding of a directory entry is a copy.\n\nMeasured, not argued. I broke `scanright`'s multibyte rewind outright (`loc2 -= ml - 1` → `loc2 -= 1`) and ran a hand-written 44-case corpus of trims, including a dozen deliberately UTF-8 ones: **44/44 passed.** The same corpus under `DS_LOCALE=C.utf8` failed 2. `aud_foundation_utf8` does not help — its three cases dump bytes with `od -c`, which is what makes them locale-independent and also what stops them from decoding anything.\n\nThe fix in this stretch is a per-case `#!locale=NAME` directive in `dscase.sh`, and `dsdiff.sh` **refuses the corpus** if the named locale is not installed rather than letting `setlocale` fall back to C in silence. That is the same rule `divergences.sh`'s header states: a classification that is too permissive is worse than none.\n\nWhat it does *not* do, and what somebody should decide: the other ~100 corpora still run under C only, and there is no second pass over them under a UTF-8 locale. `tests/.build/runall-locale.log` suggests someone has run one by hand before. A `DS_LOCALE=C.utf8` sweep is one `runall` and would say how much of the multibyte surface has ever been compared; my guess from this slice is that the answer is \"almost none of it\", and the two places that would be found first are `varvalue`'s `EXP_MBCHAR` arm and `ifsbreakup`'s wide-character splitting, neither of which this node has touched."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-16T12:17:50.824615022Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "**The plan's remaining framing for this node is wrong in one place, and a measured survey says so: `parser.rs`'s \"token buffers (286 ops)\" are not this node's work, and are mostly already done.**\n\nThe brief has carried \"then parser.rs token buffers (286 ops) if runway allows\" since the node opened. I surveyed all six of the file's heaviest functions, classifying every unsafe operation as (A) `static mut` shell state, (B) the input layer, (C) genuine C-string handling of *token text* — this node's — or (D) other:\n\n| function | A | B | **C** | D |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `command` | 7 | 1 | **1** | ~40 |\n| `readtoken1` | 2 | 8 | **0** | ~20 |\n| `parsesub` | 0 | 15 | **0** | ~10 |\n| `simplecmd` | 2 | 2 | **2** | ~8 |\n| `parsefname` | 3 | 0 | **4** | 3 |\n| `fixredir` | 0 | 0 | **6** | 0 |\n\n**`readtoken1` and `parsesub` — 41 operations, the two the phrase \"token buffers\" most obviously means — contribute zero.** Their token buffer `st.out` is already a `BString` and every write to it is a `push`, `truncate` or checked index; what is left in them is `checkkwd`/`quoteflag` and `pgetc`, which belong to `statics-that-are-not-state` and the input layer. The 286 is those two nodes' plus the `*const Syntax` pointer-identity comparisons already recorded as an independent item.\n\n**All of parser.rs's bucket C is thirteen operations in four functions**, and every one goes through one of three shapes: `wordtext_ptr(sh)`, `NodeText::as_ptr()`, or `fixredir`'s `text: *const c_char` parameter. `55a25ed` took the part that needs no callee to change. **What blocks the rest is four callees that still measure with `strlen`** — `goodname` (parser.rs, also called from eval.rs:1562), `isassignment` (also eval.rs:1184), `exec::find_builtin`, and `expand::rmescapes` — plus `fixredir` being `pub` with a second caller in `eval.rs:818`, which is the only cross-node signature coupling in the set. So the parser residue is a *callee-signature* job, not a buffer job, and it reaches into `eval.rs` and `exec.rs`.\n\nRecording this because the next agent should not spend a stretch looking for a token buffer to convert. The remaining mass for this node is in `expand.rs`, not `parser.rs`."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-16T12:18:52.476878707Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "**Third stretch: five slices landed, final gates green, and the resumption note. Master `55a25ed`, tree clean, nothing running.**\n\nLanded by fast-forward, rebasing twice as `host-owns-signals` landed under me:\n\n* `c93a96f` — the glob buffer is an index, not a cursor; `expmeta` takes slices. The `static mut globbuf`, `globb()`, `globbase()`, `globgrowto()`, `globstnputs()` all go.\n* `a25ea61` — the scan family takes offsets; `scanleft`/`scanright` become **safe functions** and stop writing a temporary NUL to read a substring. Plus the `#!locale=` harness directive.\n* `8d9232d` — `argstr`/`expari`/`casematch` stop holding the expansion buffer by pointer, leaving `_rmescapes` its only pointer-holder.\n* `143c778` — the encoder takes the bytes it encodes; `memtodest`/`mbtodest` become **safe functions**.\n* `55a25ed` — the parser's word text is a field; three readers stop pretending otherwise.\n\n**The `expmeta` coupling the last entry set up, answered and discharged.** `addfnamealt`'s `enddir` parameter existed only because `expmeta_rmescapes` wrote through a raw cursor without committing, so the candidate's length and the buffer's length were two different numbers. Committing the writes makes them one, and **the parameter is what goes** — not `n`. One statement had to be *added*, and it is the only place in the stretch where the conversion costs a line rather than saving one: when `lstat` rejects a literal tail the C returns with uncounted bytes still in spare capacity, but counted bytes must be rewound, so the failure arm gets an explicit `b.truncate(expdir_len)`. `a_rejected_tail_rewinds_the_buffer` names it.\n\n**Trend, measured by `docs/errors-are-values.md` §0.6 at every step, and attributed carefully because two nodes were reducing the same number.**\n\n```\nnode base f9dc0ec                     3075\n  after c93a96f                       3009\n  after a25ea61                       2965\n  after 8d9232d                       2955\n  after 143c778                       2937     <- all on a line with only my changes: -138\n  master 8070dd2 (their work + mine)  2906\n  after 55a25ed                       2890     <- -16\n```\n\nMine is **-154**; `host-owns-signals` reported -31 on its own line; 3075 - 154 - 31 = 2890, which is what the final tree measures, so the two attributions reconcile exactly. Do not add the two nodes' \"3075 -> N\" figures — they share a baseline. Also: **`unsafe fn` 507 -> 497, raw-pointer derefs 543 -> 499, `static mut` 49 -> 43** (five of those six are the signals node's). `expand.rs` **490 -> 353**, `parser.rs` 286 -> 269.\n\nTwo honest deductions from that number. `pmatch_slices`, `memtodest` and `mbtodest` became *safe* functions with small `unsafe` blocks inside (5 operations total: `fnmatch`, `mbrlen`, `mem::zeroed`), so 5 of the 154 are reclassification rather than removal. They are wrappers that genuinely discharge the obligation — `mbrlen`'s `n` used to be a number the caller had to get right and is now the slice's own length — but the metric cannot see the difference, so it is stated here.\n\n**Final gates, on `55a25ed`, binary `65c42db4`.**\n\n```\ncargo test -p nsh   231/0  (227 mine + 4 from the signals node)\nlib warnings        69     (73 at the node base)\nrunall              PASS=61724 FAIL=0 FLAKY=37 XFAIL=4, 116 corpora,\n                    zero .out sidecars, no STALE, no `!!`\n                    61699 (their final) + 25 (aud_parser_encode) = 61724 exactly\ndivtest             DIVERGENCE REGISTER: PASS=44 FAIL=0\nPOSIX               cases PASS=658 FAIL=53 SKIP=0, twice, byte-identical sets\nptydiff             NOT RUN -- see below\n```\n\n**On POSIX, the strongest available statement, because the raw score is not one.** Reference dash run in the same conditions minutes later scores **653/58**, and `comm` of the two failing sets says there is **not one case the port fails that the reference passes** — the port's 53 are a strict subset of the reference's 58. The `sig-kill-option-s` / `sig-kill-xsi-signal-forms` pair fails here and *also fails on reference dash*, and passes 3/3 in isolation, which is exactly the load/order-dependent behaviour the signals node diagnosed. Their quiet run scored 660/51; my machine never got below a 1-minute load of ~4 after the sweep. Not a regression, and not mine.\n\n**ptydiff was not run, and the reason corrects something I asserted too early.** I ran it once, got `PASS=0 FAIL=43`, saw the signals agent's concurrent run also failing, and wrote that my run's `rm -rf` of `ptydiff.py`'s fixed shared root had destroyed theirs. **That was causation I had not proven.** Their landed note has the measured cause: `tests/.build` is a symlink to the root checkout's, and `sandbox` refuses to create a bind mountpoint through a symlink on a read-only root — every case dies with `Can't mkdir parents`, on either agent's run, with or without a collision. It reads exactly like a catastrophic regression and is not one. The shared-run-root race I found is a real latent hazard and my one-line `mkdtemp` fix was correct, but it was not the cause and it does not help while the parent is symlinked; I reverted it rather than land an untested change to a harness that is now the signals agent's exclusively. Whoever owns `owned-words` next: **your worktree's `tests/.build` is still a symlink, so ptydiff cannot run there at all until it is a real directory with only `ref` symlinked back.** `dsdiff`/`runall` are unaffected — they scratch in `$TMPDIR` and only ever hardlink through the symlink.\n\n**Three corpora added, each mutation-checked rather than assumed.** `aud_parser_globrec` (34, the recursive glob path), `aud_parser_trim` (52, the scan family), `aud_parser_encode` (25, the encoder). Deleting the `lstat` rewind breaks 2 glob_buffer tests and 1 globrec case while the pre-existing 50-case `aud_parser_glob` still passes 50/50 — which is why globrec exists. Dropping `mbtodest`'s step-back breaks 12 of 25 encode cases; truncating the separator breaks 5.\n\n**The gate finding that outlives this node is filed as its own entry above: the sweep pins `LC_ALL=C`, so every multibyte path in the shell was differentially untested.** A mutation that broke `scanright`'s multibyte rewind outright passed 44 hand-written UTF-8 trim cases under C. `#!locale=` fixes it per case and `dsdiff.sh` now refuses a corpus naming an uninstalled locale; **the other ~113 corpora still run under C only, and nobody has ever swept them under UTF-8.**\n\n---\n\n**Resumption state and the next slice, sharpened the way the last entry sharpened `expmeta`.**\n\nRemaining in `expand.rs` (380 -> 353 over the stretch): `argstr` 43, `_rmescapes` 39, `varvalue` ~22, `expmeta` 21, `evalvar` 19, `removerecordregions` 17, `expandmeta_glob` 17.\n\n**Two of those are not this node's, and reading them is how I know.** `removerecordregions`' 17 are seventeen `ifsr()` calls — the `ifsregions` `static mut` — and they are seventeen *deliberately*: the accessor exists so that a reference to the static is never named twice at once, and the calls bracket `INTOFF`/`INTON` pairs. Collapsing them to one `let r = ifsr()` would hold a `&'static mut` across those brackets, which is the thing the pattern exists to avoid. It is `statics-that-are-not-state`'s, and it should not be counted against this node. `varvalue`'s residue is the positional-parameter vector (`shellparam.p()`, a `*mut *mut c_char`) and `lookupvar`'s return — `owned-vars`' ground.\n\n**So the next slice is `_rmescapes`, and here is the fact it turns on, which I established and did not spend.** `_rmescapes` walks with three pointers — `p` (source), `q` (destination cursor), `r` (destination base) — over *three different destinations*: the source itself (in place), the expansion buffer (`RMESCAPE_GROW`), or a caller's `Vec` (`RMESCAPE_HEAP`). That is why it resisted the treatment `expmeta` and the scan family got: there is no single base to index from.\n\nThe way through is an invariant nobody has written down: **the output never exceeds the input on the in-place path, in either build configuration.** Checked arm by arm with `FNMATCH_IS_ENABLED` at 0: `CTLQUOTEMARK` consumes 1 and writes 0; a naked backslash writes 1 for 1; the `CTLESC` arm consumes 2 and writes 2 with globbing, 1 without; both `CTLMBCHAR` arms write no more than they consume; the default is 1 for 1. And with FNMATCH *on*, the growing writes are all guarded by `globbing != 0` or by `inquotes`, which needs `globbing` — while `preglob` sets `RMESCAPE_ALLOC` whenever FNMATCH is on, so every growing call takes the allocating path with its doubled `fulllen` and the in-place path never grows.\n\nThat makes the shape available: **an in-place compaction over one `&mut [u8]` with two indices**, which is the whole function for `flag == 0`, and which the two allocating arms can reach by *first* copying the source into the destination (`Vec::extend_from_within` for `GROW`, `extend_from_slice` for `HEAP`) and then compacting there. The one case that does not fit is FNMATCH-on-plus-globbing, where the output can be twice the input — dead code today, and the decision to take is whether to carry it as a second, two-buffer path or to record it as a configuration this port does not build. **That decision is the slice's real content and it deserves a `plan/decisions/owned-data.md` entry**; the rest is mechanical. Budget it as `_rmescapes` 39 plus the `expbase`/`expdest`/`set_expdest`/`expmakestrspace` accessors it is the last caller of, which retire with it.\n\nGate it with `aud_parser_trim` and `aud_parser_encode` (both new, both locale-aware), `aud_parser_expand`, and the here-document fuzzers `aud_parser_fuzzh1`/`fuzzh2` at 700 cases each — `_rmescapes` is on the here-document delimiter path via `rmescapes_owned`. Expect `function-density` on `expand.rs` to fire again; it is at 71 and the answer has been the same three times.\n\n`measure_unsafe.sh` is worth re-creating from §0.6 rather than trusting any number. Two hazards cost me runs: cargo replays cached diagnostics for a crate it thinks is fresh, so **every** edit of `lib.rs` needs a `touch` — including the first one, because the *other* worktree shares `target/` and its cached diagnostics get replayed for yours; and `cargo build -p nsh` does **not** build the `nsh` binary, which lives in `nsh-cli`. I ran three differential corpora against a stale binary before noticing the second one."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-16T13:04:44.563729257Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "**Completion assessment. The expansion half is done and measured; the parsing half is transferred, and this entry says exactly which is which so the decision is checkable rather than asserted.**\n\n**Fourth stretch, five more slices, all landed by fast-forward:**\n\n* `1e8047c` — `_rmescapes` is a compaction over one `&mut [u8]`; `expbase`/`expdest`/`set_expdest`/`expmakestrspace` retire with it.\n* `3e0d763` — the word being expanded is a slice; `argstr`, `evalvar`, `subevalvar`, `expari`, `exptilde` take offsets.\n* `21fd5e4` — field splitting takes the word as bytes; `ifs_state` stops holding pointers.\n* `f9f8ebe` — three pointer adapters whose stated reason had evaporated.\n\n**The title's first half — \"word expansion … operate on owned bytes, not C pointers\" — is done, and here is the evidence rather than the claim.** `expand.rs` is at 242 unsafe operations, from 490 when this node opened. Every one of its 21 remaining raw-pointer dereferences is located and attributed, and **not one is a word-text walk**:\n\n| where | count | owner |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ifsr()`/`expargl()`/`expb()` bodies, `exparg`, `argbackq` | 6 | [[statics-that-are-not-state]] |\n| `varvalue`'s name + the positional-parameter vector | 5 | [[statics-that-are-not-state]] (its log inventories `shellparam` at 40 sites) |\n| `expmeta`'s `dirent64` fields, `getpwnam`'s `pw_dir` | 6 | [[unsafe-is-a-crate]] |\n| `expandmeta_glob`'s `gl_pathv` | 3 | dead: `GLOB_IS_ENABLED` is a `const bool` false, and `system.rs`'s `glob64` stub returns −1 with a test pinning it |\n| `memrchr` | 1 | dead: the C's `#ifndef HAVE_MEMRCHR` fallback, kept because the `#ifndef` is |\n\nOf the 242, **98 are the `static mut` accessors and `INTOFF`/`INTON`**, and most of the ~100 \"call to unsafe function\" operations are calls to functions that are `unsafe fn` *only* because their bodies touch one of those statics — they collapse behind that node, not this one. Both transfers are filed with these numbers on the receiving logs.\n\n**The title's second half — \"and parsing\" — is transferred, and this is the part that deserves a straight answer rather than a quiet completion.** The per-function survey filed two entries up found `parser.rs`'s \"token buffers (286 ops)\" is not this node's work: `readtoken1` and `parsesub` contribute *zero* raw handling of token text, because `st.out` has been a `BString` since `owned-strings`. `55a25ed` took the part that needed no callee to change. What is left is **thirteen operations in four functions**, and all of them go through one of three shapes — `wordtext_ptr`, `NodeText::as_ptr`, `fixredir`'s `text: *const c_char`.\n\n**They are blocked on four callee signatures, not on a buffer:** `goodname` and `isassignment` (in parser.rs, but each with a second caller in `eval.rs`), `exec::find_builtin`, and `fixredir` itself, which is `pub` with a caller at `eval.rs:818`. So finishing it means editing `eval.rs` and `exec.rs` — and `exec.rs` is [[process-model]]'s active file as of this stretch. That is why it is transferred rather than done: it is a callee-signature job in two other files, gated on another agent's tree, and it is small.\n\n**Final gates, on `f9f8ebe`, binary `5ed77efe`:**\n\n```\ncargo test -p nsh   231/0\nlib warnings        69          (73 at the node base)\nrunall              PASS=61768 FAIL=0 FLAKY=38 XFAIL=4, 118 corpora,\n                    zero .out sidecars, no STALE, no `!!`\n                    61724 + 44 new cases = 61768 exactly\ndivtest             DIVERGENCE REGISTER: PASS=44 FAIL=0\nPOSIX               cases PASS=658 FAIL=53 SKIP=0 — the failing set\n                    BYTE-IDENTICAL to the run before `_rmescapes`, the\n                    argstr cluster and the field splitter, which is the\n                    strongest thing a representation-only change can say\nptydiff             NOT RUN — off-limits to this node by coordination\n```\n\n**Trend, reconciled against the node that was reducing the same number in parallel.**\n\n```\nnode base f9dc0ec  3075\n  slices 1-4        -138   (measured on a line carrying only my changes)\n  slice 5            -16\n  _rmescapes         -48\n  argstr cluster     -43\n  field splitting    -15\n  dead adapters       -5\n                   ------\n  this node          -265\n  host-owns-signals   -31   (its own measurement, its own line)\nfinal f9f8ebe      2779 = 3075 - 265 - 31, exactly\n```\n\nAlso: **raw-pointer dereferences 543 → 472**, **`unsafe fn` declarations 507 → 491**, `static mut` 49 → 43 (five of the six are the signals node's). `expand.rs` 490 → 242 and `parser.rs` 286 → 269 — since `3e0d763`, `expand.rs` has no longer been the largest concentration of unsafe in the crate, which it had been for the whole life of the port.\n\n**Honest deduction, stated because the metric cannot see it:** seven operations moved *inside* explicit `unsafe` blocks rather than disappearing, in `pmatch_slices`, `memtodest`, `mbtodest` and `mbrlen`'s caller. Those are safe wrappers that genuinely discharge the obligation — the length `mbrlen` needs used to be a number the caller had to get right and is now the slice's own — but 7 of the 265 are reclassification, not removal.\n\n**Two coverage facts recorded rather than glossed.** The `CTLESC` arm's inner reach-back in `rmescapes_compact` survives mutation against the entire sweep and 1331 generated quote/backslash patterns, because reaching it needs a literal backslash immediately before a closing quote marker and the parser does not emit one; it is transcribed unchanged and written up in `plan/decisions/owned-data.md`. And the whole multibyte surface was untested before this node added `#!locale=`; `[[locale-sweep]]` now carries what remains of that.\n\n**Five corpora added this node, every one mutation-checked against the code it gates:** `aud_parser_globrec` (34), `aud_parser_trim` (52), `aud_parser_encode` (25), `aud_parser_tilde` (16), `aud_parser_ifs` (28) — 155 cases, of which 25 carry `#!locale=C.utf8`.\n\nMaster `f9f8ebe`, both trees clean, nothing running."
                            }

                            {
