            {
                id process-model
                title "fork, exec and wait in a library: what a Shell may do to the process"
                priority 0
                kind @refactor
                deps (no-ambient-state)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:51:23.616088533Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "**Two decisions recorded, and the reframing that made them two rather than one.**\n\n`[dec:nsh:host-owns-the-process]` (@ban) — *the library may create a process; replacing, regrouping or ending the host's is the host's to grant.*\n`[dec:nsh:fork-child-is-a-terminus]` (@property) — *a forked child never returns, and a vforked one writes no location the parent reads again.*\n\n**The question this node was given is \"which process operations belong to the Shell\", and the first honest answer is that the question is mis-posed.** The tempting formulation — a list of banned syscalls — does not survive `jobs.rs`. `setpgid` appears twice in the same fork, eleven lines apart: `jobs.rs:992` in the child, `:1079` in the parent, deliberately raced so whichever wins puts the job in its group. One of those is ordinary work; the other operates on the embedder's process. Same syscall, same function, same second.\n\nSo the line is **whose process is the object of the call**, and that is what splits the node in two: one decision about the host's process, one about the child's. `docs/api-design.md` §11.1 is the table.\n\n## What the design found that was not already known\n\n**1. `exec cmd` is not the only site that replaces the process image, and the other one has no syntax attached.** §5.4 named `eval.rs:1341` (`execcmd`). There are three `shellexec` callers, and the third is `eval.rs:1389` — `evalcommand`'s `EV_EXIT` fast path, reached from `main`'s `-c` at `shellmain.rs:199`. `dash -c 'ls'` `execve`s in place. An embedder reading the shell's manual would defend against `exec` and never see this one. The fix is not a second `Host` method: **`Shell::run` passes no `EV_EXIT`**, which leaves the optimisation to `nsh-cli` and puts it out of the API's reach — and closes `evalsubshell`'s no-fork arm (`eval.rs:717-721`) at the same time, since that is `EV_EXIT`-only too.\n\n**2. `wait3(-1)` reaps the host's children, and it is the first process-wide fact on §6's list that is the kernel's rather than libc's.** An embedder holding a `std::process::Child` gets `ECHILD` for a status now sitting in a job table it cannot see. **It is not fixable by tracking pids**, and the reason is worth keeping because it is not obvious: reaping is destructive, so the ownership test must precede the reap; `waitid(P_ALL, …, WNOWAIT)` does that, but when the only exited child is the embedder's it returns the same one forever and a blocking wait becomes a spin. Proper dispatch needs the shell to own `SIGCHLD` for the whole process, which `[dec:nsh:host-owns-signals]` forbids. Documented, like the locale.\n\nThat entry also produced a correction to how §6 has been *read*. The list acquired the shape \"libc's globals\" because its first three entries were, and because §5's `static mut` audit was the tool at hand. **Three of the seven are not libc's**: the working directory and the child pool are the kernel's, the signal inbox is this crate's. The test is now stated as \"anything one `Shell` can change that another observes, whoever stores it\" — applying the narrower one is how the child pool went unlisted through `public-api-design`, `move-state` and `host-owns-signals`.\n\n**3. `ttyfd` is already the interlock, so terminal handoff needs exactly one gate.** `xxtcsetpgrp` returns `Ok(())` when `ttyfd < 0` (`jobs.rs:351-357`) and only `setjobctl` ever sets `ttyfd`. Gating `setjobctl` gates `forkchild`'s handoff, `waitforjob`'s hand-back and `fg`'s — including the ones inside forked children, which is the right answer even though a child's `tcsetpgrp` is not an operation on the host, because a child stealing the terminal from the host's foreground group is the same theft one process away.\n\n**4. §5.4's forked-child constraint was posed too strongly, and answering it narrower is the useful result.** \"Async-signal-safe work only, no destructors, no allocator\" is unattainable after `fork` and unnecessary: a subshell *is* a shell, so it allocates because it evaluates, and `forkchild`'s `freejob` walk frees the job table's owned strings before the child runs a command. After `fork` the rule is only *it does not return*. The strong form belongs to `vfork`, where it is checkable — and was checked.\n\n**5. Twelve of twenty-one disposition sites are child-side, which shrinks `public-api`'s `Host` seam and stops it building something unsound.** `[dec:nsh:host-owns-signals]`' transfer lists `redir.rs`'s five raw `libc::signal` calls among the sites to move. They are in the here-document writer child. Routing any child-side site through `Box<dyn Host>` is an indirect call into embedder code from a forked child — the hazard the sibling decision exists to bound.\n\n## The audit, and the one line it cost\n\nThe `vfork` rule was worth having only if it was checked rather than asserted, so the prologue was walked item by item. **Five parent-visible writes expected, four already guarded** — the `mypid = 0`/`shlvl`/`forkreset`/`jobctl` block and the `sigmode` stores in `setsignal`/`ignoresig` (`trap.rs:306`, `:326`). The fifth, `forkchild`'s `mypid = pgrp = getpid()`, was not, and it is `src/jobs.c:891` transcribed faithfully.\n\nConsequence: after a foreground external command under `set -m`, the parent's `mypid` holds its child's pid; `vforkexec`'s `if mypid == 0` never re-reads it; the next `vfork` publishes a stale pid as `vforked`; and `onsig`'s `getpid() != vforked` then answers **true for the parent**, dropping any signal arriving between `vfork` returning and `set_vforked(0)`. A few instructions wide and it needs `set -m`, so nobody was going to hit it. Fixed anyway at `410e729` under `[dec:nsh:we-own-the-defects]`, and recorded at length because the value here is the method, not the bug: four guards out of five means dash's authors had the rule and applied it almost everywhere.\n\nTwo writes are permitted and **named rather than removed** — `forkchild_fatal`'s and `shellexec`'s `sh.status = …`, each immediately before its own `_exit`. The parent overwrites `sh.status` from the child's wait status in `waitforjob` before reading it, and `shellexec`'s failure path must build dash's diagnostic where dash writes it, which allocates in the shared heap whatever is done about the field. A rule with a stated exception beats a rule that is quietly false.\n\n## What was deliberately not done\n\nNo speculative refactor. The three grants need `Builder` and `Box<dyn Host>`, which is `public-api`'s, so they are recorded there with call sites rather than half-built here. `exitshell` still `_exit`s the host's process and `sh.run(b\"exec ls\")` still replaces its image; both are on `public-api`'s log with the evidence.\n\nThe syscall floor is enumerated (255 `libc::` sites, 68 symbols, 13 more hand-declared, thirteen groups) and handed to `unsafe-is-a-crate` with the finding that **the floor is not the syscalls** — `fork/exec/wait/signals/termios/fd` is 170 of 255, and the remaining 85 include the locale-dependent C library routines whose replacement is a behaviour decision. That resolves `[dec:nsh:minimal-unsafe]`'s deferred consequence, applied in the same commit."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:55:01.103836061Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T14:33:29.877953698Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "2026-08-17: The recorded conclusion that the vfork window could stay allocation-free is false after owned-word/process-argv work (and, in Rust, was not a sound invariant to expose to ordinary heap-owning code). `shellexec` constructs environment/argument/path Vec/CString values in the child. Reproducer before the fix: `nsh -c '/bin/true; /bin/true'` reliably SIGSEGV'd at the parent return from `nsh_platform::vfork_process`; GDB stopped on that return. The implementation now uses fork for the immediate-exec child, deletes the vfork marker and its signal/state exceptions, and keeps the fork-child terminus rule. The rejected `fork everywhere` alternative and vfork-specific accepted consequences in [dec:nsh:fork-child-is-a-terminus] need superseding when this work is recorded."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id public-api
                title "Implement the public API and close the surface: 35 pub mod down to what an embedder needs"
                priority 0
                kind @feat
                deps (no-ambient-state host-owns-signals public-api-design)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:47:48.64986797Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "**Obligation transferred from `move-state`: the shell's streams cannot move onto the instance until this node gives `Shell` a public constructor. The evidence is a revert, not an argument.**\n\n`move-state` surveyed `streams.rs:98 STREAMS` as one of the easiest tables in the crate — one static, `Copy` payload, two functions, one writer at `main_fn` which is the constructor site, seventeen mechanical read sites — attempted the move, and reverted it. The blocker is not the read sites and is not effort. It is that **`set` has two other callers that have no shell and cannot be given one**:\n\n* `streams::install(s) -> Borrowed` is the *embedding* surface. Its only in-crate callers are five integration tests under `crates/nsh/tests/` — `streams_embed.rs`, `errors_are_values.rs`, `glob_buffer.rs`, `expansion_buffer.rs` — which stand in for a frontend pointing the shell's I/O somewhere before running it.\n* `Borrowed::restore(self)` puts the host's descriptors back. Giving it a receiver means the drop guard carries one, which is a lifetime on a type whose whole job is to be droppable.\n\n**The decisive fact: an integration test cannot construct a `Shell`.** `context::Shell::new` is `pub(crate)` on purpose — `move-state` deleted `Shell::detached()` precisely to make \"every instance is the shell\" an invariant the type enforces rather than a convention. So the moment `streams::set` takes a receiver, five integration tests stop compiling, and there is no in-crate fix that does not make the constructor public — which would undo the invariant that node exists to establish.\n\nThat is not a test-design accident. It is the real question, and it is this node's: **does the shell's I/O belong to the process or to the instance?** `docs/api-design.md` answers it here — §7 makes `Streams` a builder input and §2 makes `Shell` publicly constructible through the builder. Once `Shell::builder().streams(..)` exists, `STREAMS` moves as a field initialiser and `install`/`restore` become the builder's business with no `set` at all.\n\n**What this node owes back.** When the builder lands, say so on `move-state`'s log (or move the field here directly, if that is cheaper once the constructor is public) — `move-state` has struck `streams` from its own table order and named this node as the dependency, so nothing will pick it up otherwise. `thread-context`'s close-out already recorded that `Streams::install` and the builder are this node's and are not to be touched by `move-state`; this is the same line, found from the other side and now with a compile failure behind it.\n\n**Also relevant when the surface closes:** `move-state` found that `docs/api-design.md` §5's baseline is stale in three ways that affect §5's field table this node will implement — `crates/nsh/src/gen/` does not exist (so every \"excluding `gen/`\" qualifier is a no-op and the real count is 107 `static mut`, not 124), five statics §5 lists are already gone to upstream nodes, and `output.rs`'s `output`/`errout`/`preverrout`/`out1`/`out2` were collapsed into a single `SHELL_IO` by `output-is-a-writer`, so the `io: ShellIo` row describes a module that no longer looks like that."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T15:01:45.470123368Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @blocker
                        text "**Obligation transferred from `move-state` (1 of 2): `io` rides with `streams`, and the existing `streams` obligation on this node should be read as covering both.** `move-state` completed with its scope narrowed; this is one of three transfers, and the evidence is here so it is not re-derived.\n\n**`output.rs`'s `SHELL_IO` cannot move for exactly the reason `streams.rs`'s `STREAMS` cannot, through the same three functions:**\n\n```\nstreams::install(s)     -> set(s) -> output::set_stream_fds(..) -> SHELL_IO\nBorrowed::restore(self) -> set(Streams::INHERIT) -> ditto\n```\n\nThe moment `SHELL_IO` is `sh.io`, `set_stream_fds` needs `&mut Shell`, so `set` does, so `install` and `restore` do. Five integration tests under `crates/nsh/tests/` call `install` (`streams_embed.rs` ×2, `expansion_buffer.rs`, `glob_buffer.rs`, `errors_are_values.rs`) and **`Shell::new` is `pub(crate)`** — deliberately, since that is the invariant `move-state` was establishing — so an integration test cannot construct one. And `Borrowed::restore` takes `self` on a type whose whole purpose is to be droppable. **They are one blocked group, not two independent rows of §5.**\n\n**Two escapes were considered and rejected; recorded so nobody spends the time again.**\n\n1. **Derive `Output.fd` from `streams()` lazily instead of pushing it.** Dead: `redir.rs:191` writes `(*previous_stderr()).fd` directly when `set -x`'s stderr is renamed across a redirection, so an `Output`'s fd is *not* always its stream's. It would also mean consulting a process-global on every write.\n2. **Let `Shell::new()` read `streams()` for its initial fds and delete `set_stream_fds`.** This nearly works — `shellmain.rs:320` calls `streams::set` *before* `Shell::new()` at `:337`, and the embedding tests install in a forked child before the shell runs — but it narrows the contract so `set` no longer moves a *live* shell's writers, and `streams.rs`'s own unit tests `set_moves_the_shells_own_io_without_touching_descriptor_one` and `the_shells_writers_follow_the_streams` exist to assert that it does.\n\n**When the builder lands, escape (2) is the shape.** §7 makes `Streams` a builder input and §2 makes `Shell` publicly constructible; at that point `install`/`set`/`restore` become the builder's business, `set_stream_fds` disappears, and `sh.io` is a field initialiser like every other table.\n\n**Two further things `io` needs, which are this node's to decide and are not the builder.**\n\n* **The `out: *mut Output` seam has a soundness question, not just an aliasing one.** Six functions take one (`jobs.rs`'s `showjob`/`showjobs`/`showpipe`/`outcmd`, `eval.rs`'s `eprintlist`, `builtins/type.rs`'s `describe_command`), reached from three origins. **Nine sites hold that pointer live across a `&mut Shell` call.** Today that is sound because the pointer comes from `addr_of_mut!` on a *static*; taken from `&mut sh.io` instead, every later reborrow of the shell puts its provenance in question under Stacked/Tree Borrows. It would compile and might be UB. The recommended fix is a **destination selector** (`Stdout | Stderr | PreviousStderr`) resolved against `&mut sh.io` inside the callee, which removes the pointer, the aliasing and the provenance question together.\n* **`sh_warnx` writes to `stderr()` and holds no receiver**, so `io` is also entangled with the diagnostic-spine obligation in the next entry. The two want one answer.\n\n**Good news, both narrowing the risk:** `trap.rs`'s `onsig` does **not** touch `SHELL_IO` or any `Output`, so `io` is free of the blocker holding up `traps`; and `jobs.rs`'s `forkchild` does not either, so it avoids the vfork-constrained path. The one vfork-adjacent write is `exec.rs:262`'s failure report, which reaches unbuffered `stderr` and so takes the direct-write branch."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T15:02:12.524575431Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @blocker
                        text "**Obligation transferred from `move-state` (2 of 2): `commandname`, `arg0` and `errlinno` need §3.3's diagnostic sink answered, and they need it together.**\n\nAll three are read by **one function for one purpose**: `error.rs:484 sh_warnx` builds the `sh: 17: cd: ` prefix. `arg0` is the `sh`, `errlinno` the `17`, `commandname` the `cd`. None should move alone, and moving any of them means `sh_warnx` takes a receiver.\n\n**Why that is expensive, and why `move-state`'s own trick does not work here.** `sh_warnx` is called by `report`, which is called by `sh_error_value` — **56 call sites in 24 files**, the crate's last unthreaded spine. `move-state` avoided exactly that threading for `$?` by having the `Error` carry the status to whichever frame catches it (`2d506ab`, `58fc346`). **That trick does not generalise**, and the reason is worth stating precisely:\n\n> A value can carry state to its **catch**, but it cannot carry a **prefix** to its **point of writing**. The status is consumed after the error is caught, so the error can transport it. The prefix is consumed *while the diagnostic is being written*, inside the raise, where there is no catch frame to defer to.\n\nSo the options are two, and neither is `move-state`'s to pick:\n\n1. **Thread the diagnostic spine.** `sh_warnx` → `report` → `sh_error_value`, 56 sites in 24 files, taking `&Shell` — shared, since it only reads the prefix, so the standing idiom applies and split borrows should be few. Mechanical and large.\n2. **Give the diagnostic its own sink**, which §3.3 already contemplates as `on_diagnostic`. If the prefix is assembled by whoever owns the sink rather than by `sh_warnx`, all three statics become the sink's and the spine never needs a receiver.\n\n**(2) is probably right**, which is the argument for not starting (1) speculatively — it is a large mechanical change that (2) would make unnecessary. This is raised as a question against §3.3 rather than as work to schedule.\n\n**Note the coupling to the `io` entry above:** `sh_warnx` also writes to `stderr()`, so whichever way this is answered constrains how `io` moves. One decision covers both.\n\n**Also owed to this node: six corrections to `docs/api-design.md` §5, which `move-state` accumulated and which this node owns the document for.**\n\n1. `crates/nsh/src/gen/` does not exist — every \"excluding `gen/`\" qualifier is a no-op, and the baseline of \"124 crate-owned declarations\" is a loose grep figure; the real count was **107**.\n2. Five statics §5 lists were already absorbed upstream (`exec.rs lastcmdentry`, `jobs.rs njobs`, and `output.rs`'s five collapsed into `SHELL_IO`).\n3. §5.2's `commandname` line is stale — it describes the C's `char *`; the port owns the bytes, and §5's placement was right.\n4. §5's note that the `vars` backing buffers \"move together\" understates it: the group was **self-referential**, and `defifsvar`/`defoptindvar`/`defpathvar` never move at all — they are immutable statics.\n5. `streams` — and `io` with it — need the builder first.\n6. **§5's table is not a complete inventory of shell state.** Nine per-shell statics were missing from it and have since moved: `cd.rs`'s `curdir`/`physdir`, `mail.rs`'s `mailtime`/`changed`, `expand.rs`'s `ifsmap`/`ncifs`/`wcifs`/`ifsmb0len`, and `histedit.rs`'s `displayhist`. A reader finishing §5's rows would have believed the job done with nine pieces of per-shell state still process-global. Four more (`shellmain.rs`'s `rootpid`, `mypid`, `shlvl`, `dash_errno`) are process identity and belong in §6 beside the locale and `getopt` limits it already documents."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:25:30.560162222Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @blocker
                        text "**Obligation transferred from `host-owns-signals`: the *disposition* half of \"the frontend claims signal dispositions\" is yours, because it cannot exist before the builder. The *delivery* half landed there. The seam is measured, not estimated.**\n\n`host-owns-signals`' title has two halves and only one of them could land under it, for the same reason `io`+`streams` transferred here from `move-state`.\n\n**What landed there — delivery.** `crates/nsh/src/siginbox.rs`: a process-wide `static SINK` of atomics holding the trap-presence mirror `onsig` reads (`[AtomicBool; NSIG]`, `trap[n].is_some()`) and `vforked` (`AtomicI32`). `onsig` now reads only the sink, which is what let `trap`/`ptrap`/`trapcnt`/`sigmode` become `sh.traps: TrapTable`. `TrapTable::set` is the sole writer of a slot and takes a `&SignalsBlocked` witness so a write outside a blocked-mask bracket does not compile.\n\n**What is left for you — disposition, i.e. §5.4's `Host` trait.** The order is forced: `Host::attach` needs `Shell` to own a `Box<dyn Host>`, which needs §2's `Builder`, which is this node's. The seam, counted in the source rather than guessed:\n\n* seven `trap::setsignal` call sites — `trap.rs` (the `mkinit` fragment, `clear_traps`, `exitshell`'s three), `builtins/trap.rs` in `trapcmd`, and three in `jobs.rs` (`setjobctl`, `forkchild`'s job-control arm, `forkchild`'s reset);\n* two `trap::ignoresig` — both in `forkchild`'s `FORK_BG` arm;\n* the two `sigaction` calls inside `setsignal` itself, the query and the install;\n* `redir.rs`'s five raw `libc::signal` calls in the here-document child.\n\n**Policy stays the library's, mechanism becomes the host's** — which is what §5.4 already says. `setsignal` decides *which* disposition a signal gets, including the `mflag`/SIGTSTP/SIGTTIN/SIGTTOU exception and the \"don't hard ignore these\" carve-out; only the act of installing it moves.\n\n**Three things to inherit with it.**\n\n1. **The sink is storage without ownership.** It is a plain `static` of atomics, deliberately not a `static mut`, so what it absorbed left the count rather than moving within it. Giving it the `Arc`, `Host::attach` and a `Shell` field is this node's — and note that the `Arc` buys *shareability*, not per-shell-ness.\n2. **`docs/api-design.md` §6 now lists the signal inbox** among the process-wide facts beside the locale, `strtok` and `getopt`, and §6's honest-statement sentence was rewritten because \"two `Shell` values share nothing this crate owns\" was false once the inbox existed. A per-shell disposition table is not available to design toward; do not try.\n3. **`error.rs:61 suppressint` is no longer handler state** and §5.1 now says so. Since `errors-are-values` step F `onsig` does not read it, so it is ordinary shell state that could become a field — yours or `errors-are-values`', but it should stop being counted against the signal group either way.\n\nCoverage that exists to protect this when you move it: the seventeen pty cases in `tests/harness/ptydiff.py` (the job-control block), which are the only automated coverage that delivers a real signal — the 61,592-case differential corpus sends none and has no controlling terminal."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:49:59.416615542Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "**`process-model` closing: four of your sub-questions are resolved, three obligations are added, and one of the resolutions stops you building something unsound.** Decisions at `ccca396`: `[dec:nsh:host-owns-the-process]` and `[dec:nsh:fork-child-is-a-terminus]`; `docs/api-design.md` gains §11 and corrections to §5.4 and §6.\n\n## Resolved — take these as settled\n\n**1. `may_replace_process` has two call sites, and the second one has no syntax.** §5.4 names `exec cmd`. There are three `shellexec` callers: `jobs.rs:1160` (inside a vforked child — the point of the exercise), `builtins/exec.rs:48` (the `exec` builtin), and **`eval.rs:1389` — `evalcommand`'s `EV_EXIT` fast path**, which `execve`s the *last command of the script* in place. It is reached from `main`'s `-c` at `shellmain.rs:199`, so `dash -c 'ls'` replaces its own image with no `exec` written anywhere.\n\n> **The constraint on you is one line: `Shell::run` passes no `EV_EXIT`.** `nsh-cli` still does, so dash's behaviour is preserved exactly where it is observable and the API cannot reach the site at all. This is cheaper than a second `Host` method and it is not optional — a `run` built naively on the `-c` path replaces the embedder's image on `sh.run(b\"ls\")`.\n\nSecond effect, free: `evalsubshell`'s no-fork arm (`eval.rs:717-721`) is `EV_EXIT`-only, so from `run` the shell never calls `forkreset` in its own process either. One constraint closes two holes.\n\n**2. The forked-child constraint §5.4 handed to `process-model` is answered, and narrower than it was posed.** §5.4 said \"async-signal-safe work only, no destructors, no allocator\". That is **unattainable and unnecessary after `fork`**: a subshell is a shell, so it allocates because it evaluates, and `forkchild`'s `freejob` walk frees the job table's owned strings before the child runs a command. What holds after `fork` is only *it does not return*. The strong form holds after **`vfork`**, stated as \"writes no location the parent reads again\", and it has been audited line by line — one violation found (`forkchild`'s `mypid`, inherited from `src/jobs.c:891`), fixed at `410e729`, two writes permitted and named. §5.4's paragraph is corrected in place.\n\n**3. `[dec:nsh:host-owns-signals]`' transferred seam is nine sites, not twenty-one — and building the other twelve would be unsound.** Counted: **21 disposition call sites, 12 of them in a child the library just forked.**\n\n* Child-side, stays in the library: `forkchild`'s `setsignal(SIGTSTP)`/`setsignal(SIGTTOU)` (`jobs.rs:1020-1021`), its `ignoresig(SIGINT)`/`ignoresig(SIGQUIT)` (`:1023-1024`), its `setsignal(SIGINT/SIGQUIT/SIGTERM)` (`:1045-1047`), and the here-document writer's five raw `libc::signal` calls (`redir.rs:476-480`).\n* Host-side, yours: `setjobctl`'s three (`jobs.rs:478-480`), `setinteractive`'s three (`trap.rs:491-493`), `trapcmd`'s (`builtins/trap.rs:111`), `mkinit_init`'s `SIGCHLD` (`trap.rs:149`), and `onint`'s `signal(SIGINT, SIG_DFL)` before it re-raises (`error.rs:274`) — which §3.4 already assigns to the frontend.\n* `clear_traps`'s loop (`trap.rs:188`) is on both paths, via `forkreset`'s two callers; resolution 1 makes it child-side-only from `run`.\n\n**The reason this matters is not the saving.** Routing a child-side site through `Box<dyn Host>` is an indirect call into embedder code, made in a forked child, and in the here-document case in a child of a possibly-multithreaded host. The transfer's phrasing (\"`redir.rs`'s five raw `libc::signal` calls\" listed among the seam) reads as an instruction to move them. Do not.\n\n**4. Your §7 bet is compatible, and one thing about it is worth stating because it looks like a hazard and is not.** §7 says external commands are the easy part because the forked child can `dup2` the logical-to-real map into place immediately before `execve`. That holds on the `vfork` path too: **`vfork` shares the address space, not the descriptor table** (Linux uses `CLONE_VM|CLONE_VFORK` without `CLONE_FILES`), so a `dup2` in a vforked child is the child's alone. Nothing in `[dec:nsh:fork-child-is-a-terminus]` restricts fd work in a child; the rule is about *memory the parent reads*, and the fd table is not that.\n\n## New obligations\n\n**A. Job control becomes a grant, and it is a builder input.** `setjobctl(1)` performs three operations on the *host's* process: `setpgid(0, rootpid)` (`jobs.rs:482`), `tcsetpgrp` (`:483`), and on the way there possibly `killpg(0, SIGTTIN)` (`:452`), which stops the host and every sibling. Default off.\n\nThe interlock is already there and you should use it rather than adding a second gate: `xxtcsetpgrp` returns `Ok(())` when `ttyfd < 0` (`jobs.rs:351-357`), and **only `setjobctl` ever sets `ttyfd`**. So gating `setjobctl` gates `forkchild`'s handoff (`:994`), `waitforjob`'s hand-back (`:1211`) and `fg`'s (`builtins/fg.rs:85`) with it.\n\n*Open, and recorded as §11.5 rather than decided:* whether a builder flag is the right granularity, since `set -m` is executed by the *script*, and `optschanged` reaches `setjobctl` from `poplocalvars` restoring a `local -` set. A flag makes `set -m` silently ineffective in an ungranted shell. The corpus cannot see the difference — it has no controlling terminal, so `jobctl` stays 0 in *both* shells there today — so this needs a ptydiff case when you build it, plus a decision on whether an ungranted `set -m` warns the way `can't access tty` does.\n\n**B. `exitshell` is the one `_exit` that has to become a return, and the other three stay.** `trap.rs:562` ends the *host's* process. `shellmain::exit_from_child`, `jobs::forkchild_fatal` and `redir.rs:483` all end a child the library forked and are correct — `[dec:nsh:fork-child-is-a-terminus]` says so, so no `Host` method is needed for any of them. §5.4's \"terminating is not on the trait\" is right and now has the site list behind it.\n\n**C. Two things go in the crate docs, not in a type.**\n\n* **The child-process pool.** `wait3(status, flags, NULL)` (`jobs.rs:1412`) is `waitpid(-1)` and reaps *any* child of the process. An embedder holding a `std::process::Child` gets `ECHILD` for a status now sitting in a job table it cannot see. Not fixable by tracking pids — reaping is destructive, and the only peek-without-reap primitive (`waitid(P_ALL, …, WNOWAIT)`) returns the same foreign child forever and turns the blocking wait into a spin; dispatching properly would need the shell to own `SIGCHLD` for the whole process, which `[dec:nsh:host-owns-signals]` forbids. §6 now lists it.\n* **The fork precondition.** `fork` from a multithreaded host carries only the calling thread into the child, and the library's children allocate before they exec — or never exec at all. Same caveat as `Command::pre_exec`, same cause, not removable.\n\n**D. `Shell::drop` must neither wait nor kill, and that is a promise rather than an omission.** A foreground job is waited to completion before `run` returns; a background job is not, and `sh -c 'sleep 10 &'` returns with the child alive exactly as dash does. A `Drop` that waited would block the host; one that killed would exceed the grant. Write it down so nobody adds one later as a tidiness fix.\n\n## One thing that is not yours and is worth knowing\n\nPrompt reaping needs a `SIGCHLD` disposition (`trap.rs:148-149` installs one unconditionally at startup, and `waitproc`'s blocking arm is `sigsuspend` on it, `jobs.rs:1437`). So the wait design and the `Host` trait are one design: a host that grants no `SIGCHLD` handler gets a shell that can still block in `wait3` but cannot notice a background job finishing between commands. Worth a sentence on the trait's docs when you write it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:01:55.278771146Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "**Survey, and the order the work goes in. Two of the transferred questions are answered by measurement before any code moves, and one of the answers contradicts the recommendation it inherited.**\n\nMeasured at `ad9d479`. 35 `pub mod` in `lib.rs`; `nsh-cli` names exactly two of them (`nsh::shellmain`, `nsh::streams`) and the four integration tests name the same two. The surface closure is therefore *small* — 33 modules with no external caller — and its risk is entirely in the mechanical edit, which is why it stays last and rebase-adjacent.\n\n## The diagnostic-sink question, answered against its own recommendation\n\n`move-state` transferred this as a choice between (1) thread the spine, 56 sites in 24 files, and (2) give the diagnostic its own sink so the spine never needs a receiver — and recommended (2) precisely to avoid (1).\n\n**They were never alternatives, and the measurement says (1) is much cheaper than it was costed.**\n\nNot alternatives, because §3.2 already fixes `report` as `pub(crate) fn report(&mut self, e: Error) -> Error` and requires the write to happen *at the raise point* — the interleaving of diagnostics with command output is what `dscase.sh`'s `2>&1` merge puts under test in all 61,724 cases. A sink that assembled the prefix somewhere else would have to defer the write to wherever the sink is polled, which is the one thing §3.2 forbids. And the write needs `&mut` on the stderr `Output`, so even a sink that owned the prefix would not free `report` from a receiver. What the sink genuinely replaces is not the receiver — it is the three *statics*, which become its fields.\n\nCheaper, because `thread-context` already put a `&mut Shell` on every execution path. Counted over the 66 call sites of `sh_error_value`/`report`/`sh_warnx` outside `error.rs`, by matching each site to its enclosing `fn` signature:\n\n* **45 sites already have a receiver in scope.** They cost one argument each.\n* **21 do not**, and all 21 are leaf helpers whose callers do: `arith_yacc::yyerror`, `fc::scan_options` ×2 / `str_to_event` ×2, `printf::emit_field` / `check_conversion`, `test::syntax`, `cd::getpwd`, `eval::EvalState::new` ×2, `expand::varunset`, `jobs::xtcsetpgrp`, `mystring::badnum`, `options::next` ×2, `redir::{sh_open_fail, sh_dup2, sh_pipe, savefd}`.\n\nSo the real shape is ~66 argument insertions and ~13 leaf signatures, not 56 signature changes propagating upward. That is a batched mechanical slice, not a research problem, and it is the slice that dissolves the `io` blocker as a side effect: once `report` has `&mut Shell`, it writes through `sh.io.stderr()` and `SHELL_IO` has no unthreaded reader left.\n\n**Six of the 66 sites are in `expand.rs` (3), `parser.rs` (2) and `mystring.rs` (1), which another agent holds for `owned-words`.** Those six defer to the same late, rebase-adjacent slice as the surface closure; the free-function `error::report` stays alive for them until then, so the statics move last rather than the spine landing last.\n\n## The io+streams cluster: escape (2) is the shape, and it needs no builder\n\n`move-state` recorded escape (2) — `Shell::new()` reads the streams for its initial fds, `set_stream_fds` deletes — as blocked on the builder, because five integration tests call `install` and cannot construct a `Shell`. **They do not need to.** `main_fn` already takes `streams: Streams` as a parameter (`shellmain.rs:320`), and all four test files reach it: three call `install(..)` and then pass `streams::streams()`, which after a successful `install` is `Streams::INHERIT` — `streams_embed.rs:154` already writes that constant literally. The two `set`-mode cases (`streams_embed.rs:62`, `:87`) are passing a `Streams` value to `main_fn` through a global instead of through the parameter that is already there.\n\nSo the constructor takes the streams (`Shell::new(s)`), `io` and `streams` are field initialisers, and `STREAMS` / `streams()` / `set()` / `set_stream_fds()` all delete. `install` keeps only the descriptor dance and returns `Borrowed` with no shell in sight — which is what lets it stay `pub` for a frontend while §2.1's argument for retiring it (the per-instance fd table) remains future work. The two unit tests escape (2) was rejected over (`set_moves_the_shells_own_io_without_touching_descriptor_one`, `the_shells_writers_follow_the_streams`) are rewritten to assert the new contract against a constructed shell; that is the sanctioned deviation and it is exactly what this node's log predicted it would be.\n\n## Order\n\n1. **Destination selector.** `Dest::{Stdout, Stderr, PreviousStderr}` replaces `out: *mut Output` in the six functions the transfer names (`jobs::{showjob, showjobs, showpipe, outcmd}`, `eval::eprintlist`, `builtins/type::describe_command`), resolved against `&mut sh.io` inside the callee. Removes the pointer, the aliasing and the provenance question together, before `io` becomes a field and makes the provenance question real. Consumes unsafe.\n2. **The diagnostic spine.** `Shell::report`, `Diagnostics { arg0, errlinno, commandname }` as a field, `on_diagnostic` with it. Batched by area; the three forbidden files deferred.\n3. **`io: ShellIo` becomes a field.** 59 read sites of `output::{stdout, stderr, previous_stderr}`.\n4. **Streams onto the instance.** Escape (2), above.\n5. **`ExitStatus` and `Signal`**, and the builtin signature's completion.\n6. **The `Host` trait**, parent-side nine sites only — `process-model`'s resolution 3 is a hard bound, and the twelve child-side sites are not touched. Job control as a grant, with §11.5's ptydiff case.\n7. **`Shell::run`** with no `EV_EXIT` (`process-model` resolution 1, non-negotiable), `exitshell`'s `_exit` becomes a return, `Builder`, `Source`, `has_exited`, `take_captured_*`.\n8. **`api.rs` replaced by the real thing**, `examples/embed.rs` compiles and runs against it, the `api-sketch` feature is removed from `Cargo.toml` (this node's own manifest work).\n9. **Surface closure**, immediately after a rebase, minutes-small."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:02:00.742444928Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
