                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T14:55:21.768250747Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "**Stretch 1 done: the io+streams cluster is closed and the diagnostic spine is threaded. Four commits, `081a106`..`deb7623`. Resumption note at the bottom — the node stays Doing, and steps 5-9 of the recorded order are untouched.**\n\n## What resolved, against the recorded evidence\n\n**The `*mut Output` seam (`081a106`).** `output::Dest { Stdout, Stderr, PreviousStderr }`, exactly the destination selector the transfer recommended. Six functions lost their pointer parameter; there are now zero `*mut Output` parameters outside `output.rs`. The transfer's soundness claim was right and worth restating precisely: while the pointer came from `addr_of_mut!` on a *static* it was sound, because no reborrow of the shell can invalidate a pointer that never came from one — the hazard begins the moment it comes from `&mut sh.io`. `eval.rs`'s `set -x` block was the worst case, holding one live across five reborrows.\n\n**The diagnostic spine (`c9c2434`) — resolved *against* the recommendation it inherited, and the argument is in the commit.** `move-state` posed (1) thread the spine and (2) give the diagnostic its own sink, and recommended (2) to avoid (1). They were never alternatives: §3.2 already fixes `report` as `&mut self` and requires the write *at the raise point*, so a sink assembling the prefix elsewhere would defer the write, which is the one thing §3.2 forbids; and the write needs `&mut` on the stderr `Output` regardless. What the sink replaces is the statics, not the receiver. The threading was also mis-costed at \"56 sites in 24 files\": measured, **45 of 66 sites already held a receiver** and the 21 that did not were leaves. The transitive closure converged in five rounds at 26 functions.\n\n`errlinno` and `commandname` are `EvalState` fields. **`arg0` deliberately did not move with them** — it is `$0` before it is a prefix, `expand.rs` reads it as one, and §5 puts it in the options row. The transfer's \"all three together\" was about answering the sink question once, which is what happened.\n\n**io + streams (`ecfd861`) — one blocked group, closed, and the builder was not on the path.** The recorded blocker was that `streams::set` had two callers with no shell and `Shell::new` was `pub(crate)`. **The five integration cases never needed to construct a `Shell`:** `main_fn` has taken a `Streams` argument since `[dec:nsh:host-owns-streams]` landed, three cases were reading `streams::streams()` back out of a global that `install` had just written (which is `Streams::INHERIT` — `streams_embed.rs:154` already wrote the constant literally), and two were passing a value *through* the global to reach a parameter already there. So `Shell::new(streams)` takes it, `io` is the initialiser beside it, and `STREAMS`/`streams()`/`set()`/`SHELL_IO`/`stdout()`/`stderr()`/`previous_stderr()`/`set_stream_fds()` all deleted. That is escape (2), as this log predicted.\n\n**Deviation, argued not silent:** escape (2) was rejected by `move-state` because `set` moved a *live* shell's writers and two unit tests asserted it. Nothing in the crate ever did that — `main_fn` called `set` before `Shell::new` — so the tests now assert the constructor's contract, which is the property that was load bearing. Recorded in `streams.rs` beside the tests.\n\nThree functions stopped writing and started rendering, because each is reached from a walk that holds the shell borrowed while the write wants `sh.io`: `alias::printalias`, `hash`'s `printentry`, `output::freestdout`.\n\n**Docs (`deb7623`).** All six §5/§6 corrections applied. §2.1 corrected: `install` does *not* go yet — that sentence rested on the per-instance fd table, which §10 calls the largest bet in the document and which is not built. §3.3 records the sink answer.\n\n## Gates\n\n`cargo test -p nsh` 231/0 and warnings 69 at every commit — the recorded baseline, and the warning *set* diffed against it rather than just the count (that caught three unused imports). Targeted differentials with `$0` normalised: 25 for `Dest`, 44 for the spine, 27 for io+streams. Full sweep twice, **PASS=61768 FAIL=0 FLAKY=26 XFAIL=4**, no STALE, identical both times. **ptydiff PASS=60 FAIL=0 FLAKY=0** — the gate that mattered for the streams move, since it is the only coverage on a real terminal.\n\nNote on the sweep baseline: PASS is 61768 where this node was briefed with 61724. The corpus grew under `owned-words`; FAIL=0 and XFAIL=4 are the figures that carry.\n\n## Trend\n\n`static mut` declarations **43 → 39**. `unsafe fn` **494 → 487**. Unsafe ops at the stretch's base were 2779 (measured by deleting `#![allow(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)]`: 2848 warnings total, 69 without) — not re-measured at the end, and the honest reason is that it needs the same command at both ends in the same hour, which is the discipline `unsafe-is-a-crate` recorded and I did not spend the run on. The `io`/`streams` deletions removed nine mutable-static accessor funnels, so it should have fallen; do not quote a figure that was not measured.\n\nNote the briefed baseline said `static mut` 43 and `unsafe fn` 499; the declaration count matched exactly, the `unsafe fn` count measured 494 at `ab132ba`. `owned-words` landed in between.\n\n## Resumption note — what remains, in order\n\nSteps 1-4 of the recorded order are done. **Steps 5-9 are untouched**, and none is blocked:\n\n5. **`ExitStatus` and `Signal`**, and the builtin signature's completion. Note the shape already deviates from `[dec:nsh:public-surface]`'s `fn(&mut Shell, &[&BStr]) -> Result<ExitStatus, Error>`: `builtins/mod.rs` returns `Result<Flow, Error>` and documents why (`exit` is a builtin, and four builtins re-enter evaluation), which is a *better* answer than the recorded one. Reconcile deliberately rather than \"completing\" the signature to something worse.\n6. **The `Host` trait** (§5.4), parent-side **nine** sites only. `process-model`'s resolution 3 is a hard bound: the twelve child-side sites must not be routed through `Box<dyn Host>`. Job control as a grant, plus §11.5's ptydiff case for `set -m` in a granted vs ungranted shell.\n7. **`Shell::run`** with **no `EV_EXIT`** — non-negotiable, or `sh.run(b\"ls\")` replaces the embedder's image. Then `exitshell`'s `_exit` becomes a return, `Builder`, `Source`, `has_exited`, `take_captured_*`, and `Shell::drop` neither waits nor kills.\n8. **`api.rs` replaced by the real thing**; `examples/embed.rs` compiles *and runs*; the `api-sketch` feature comes out of `Cargo.toml`.\n9. **Surface closure**, immediately after a rebase, minutes-small. **Now measured and small:** `nsh-cli` names exactly two of the 35 `pub mod` (`shellmain`, `streams`) and the four integration tests name the same two. 33 modules have no external caller.\n\nTwo things to carry into step 7 that this stretch touched: `Shell::new` is still `pub(crate)` and still the one constructor, so the invariant `move-state` established is intact and the builder grows on top of it rather than around it; and `exec.rs:262`'s vfork-adjacent `report` now goes through `sh.report`, which writes only to unbuffered stderr and so still takes the direct-write branch — check that again if `report` ever gains a buffered path."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:16:34.795990165Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "RESUMPTION NOTE — stretch 2 (supersedes stretch 1's for the step order)\n\nLanded this stretch, all gated:\n  562e5c8  feat: ExitStatus and Signal (status.rs; step 5)\n  1b5440f  refactor: exitshell returns instead of _exit (obligation B)\n  5393768  refactor: api.rs re-exports the two real types instead of copying them\n\nGate state at 5393768: cargo test -p nsh 234/0 (231 baseline + 3 from\nstatus.rs), warnings 69 — both unchanged from the stretch's start. Full\ndifferential PASS=61768 FAIL=0 FLAKY=32 XFAIL=4 STALE=0 and pty PASS=60\nFAIL=0 FLAKY=1, run against 1b5440f, which is the only one of the three\nthat can reach a corpus-visible path.\n\nTwo obligations discharged. A: Error::Interrupted carries a Signal, which\n§3.4 said it would once Signal existed. B: exitshell's _exit became a\nreturn, so [dec:nsh:host-owns-the-process]'s \"ending the host's\" clause is\nnow answered everywhere. The three remaining _exits are all fork-child\ntermini and are correct.\n\nTHE RECORDED STEP ORDER IS WRONG AND SHOULD BE 7 BEFORE 6. The node log\nhad step 6 (the Host trait) before step 7 (Builder + run), with the note\nthat \"Host::attach needs Shell to own a Box<dyn Host>, which needs §2's\nBuilder\". That dependency is real and it points the other way: there is\nnowhere to put a host until Builder exists, because Shell::new(streams) is\nthe only constructor and widening it to Shell::new(streams, host) is the\nshape Builder exists to replace. Do Builder first, then hang Host off it.\nStep 5's experience supports this: the streams move needed the\nconstructor, not the builder, and this one genuinely needs the builder.\n\nNext slice, in order:\n  1. Builder: streams, argv, env, host slot, options, cwd, diagnostic\n     hook (§2). Shell::new(streams) becomes\n     Builder::new().streams(s).build(); there are ~12 in-crate callers,\n     nearly all in options.rs tests.\n  2. Host (step 6): the trait, then the nine parent-side sites from\n     process-model's resolution 3 — setjobctl's three (jobs.rs:478-480),\n     setinteractive's three (trap.rs:491-493), trapcmd's\n     (builtins/trap.rs:111), mkinit_init's SIGCHLD (trap.rs:149), and\n     onint's signal(SIGINT, SIG_DFL) (error.rs:274). The child-side 12\n     stay. clear_traps' loop (trap.rs:188) is on both paths and needs the\n     split.\n  3. Source + Shell::run (§4.2's input-stack push/unwind-floor contract).\n     This is the big one; EV_EXIT removal is part of it.\n\nWatch out for, when doing Builder/run: main's `exit:` arm must stay INSIDE\nmain's dispatch loop. The comment at shellmain.rs records why — a subshell\nforked from inside an EXIT trap raises into that frame and leaves through\ngoto exit — and an earlier attempt to lift it out killed such a child with\nRust's panic status 101. 1b5440f keeps the call in place and carries the\nstatus out in a captured Option checked before the handler; the check must\nstay before the handler, or exitreset runs twice.\n\nAlso: main_fn returns now, so any caller that forks and then calls it must\nend the child itself. The four integration tests were updated; a fifth\nwould need the same.\n\napi.rs is a compiled todo!() sketch (~836 lines) whose module doc now\nnames which types are real. Each landed type should delete its sketch copy\nand re-export, as 5393768 did, rather than leaving two copies for the\ncompiler to check independently. When the list is empty the module goes\nand the re-exports move to lib.rs."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T20:48:32.813289837Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "RESUMPTION NOTE — stretch 3 (supersedes stretch 2's \"next slice\" list)\n\nLanded this stretch, all gated:\n  794d069  feat: Builder (streams/arg0/args/env/inherit_env/option/cwd)\n  837ae94  feat: Host trait, Disposition, SignalSink, NoHost, Builder::host\n\nGate state at 837ae94: cargo test -p nsh 244/0 (231 baseline + 3 status +\n6 builder + 4 host), warnings 69 unchanged, differential PASS=61768 FAIL=0\nSTALE=0, pty PASS=60 FAIL=0 (run at 794d069).\n\nNEW STANDING GATE: `cargo check -p nsh --features api-sketch --examples`.\nCommit 5393768 broke it (it made ExitStatus's field private while\napi.rs:810 still wrote `ExitStatus(2)`) and it went unnoticed for two\ncommits because the usual three gates do not compile that feature. Fixed\nin 837ae94. RUN IT ON EVERY SLICE FROM NOW ON — it is the only thing that\ncompiles examples/embed.rs.\n\nNext slice, in order:\n  1. Route the nine parent-side signal sites through `sh.host`:\n     setjobctl's three (jobs.rs:478-480), setinteractive's three\n     (trap.rs:491-493), trapcmd's (builtins/trap.rs:111), mkinit_init's\n     SIGCHLD (trap.rs:149), onint's signal(SIGINT, SIG_DFL) (error.rs:274).\n     The child-side 12 (forkchild's 4+3, here-doc's 5) STAY — they run\n     after fork, where the child is a terminus. clear_traps' loop\n     (trap.rs:188) is on both paths and needs the split.\n     nsh-cli grows a ProcessHost doing exactly what dash does today, and\n     Shell::new's NoHost default must NOT reach the frontend or every\n     signal behaviour changes at once. This slice IS corpus-visible: full\n     sweep + pty are mandatory, and pty is the one that will catch it\n     (^C, ^Z, job control).\n  2. Source + Shell::run (§4.2's input-stack push/unwind-floor contract),\n     no EV_EXIT. The biggest remaining piece.\n  3. api.rs dedup of Builder/Host/Disposition/SignalSink + embed.rs\n     against real types + remove the `api-sketch` feature from\n     Cargo.toml. BLOCKED ON (2), not on (1): re-exporting the real\n     Builder makes .build() return the real Shell, and embed.rs calls\n     sh.run() and sh.set_var() on the next line. Do not attempt the dedup\n     before run/set_var exist — it was attempted and correctly deferred\n     twice already.\n  4. Surface closure (minutes-small, rebase-adjacent).\n\nDesign facts established this stretch, so they are not re-derived:\n - Shell::new builds an EMPTY shell; all initial state comes from\n   init::init + options::procargs. Builder::build calls init_from and\n   then applies its settings; it deliberately does NOT call procargs,\n   because procargs wipes all 18 option flags to the tri-state 2 on entry\n   and then resolves iflag/mflag from tty-ness. A builder-set option\n   applied before procargs would be destroyed.\n - The environment split is by SOURCE not by rewriting the import:\n   var::EnvSource::{Process, Explicit}. Process keeps VTEXTFIXED (the\n   shell borrows the process's own environ bytes forever, which is what\n   makes a built sh identical to an execve'd one); Explicit copies.\n   mkinit_env_pairs is shared by the Explicit branch and the\n   inherit_env+env compose path.\n - There is no \"set option by name or letter\" helper in dash: minus_o\n   (long names, doubles as `set -o` listing) and setoption (letters, ksh\n   -V/-E hack) are both private and both shaped by the command line.\n   options::set_option_by_name is a third entry point, and deliberately\n   does not call optschanged — the builder calls it once over the\n   finished set.\n - $0 is STILL a process-global static (options::arg0). Two shells in one\n   process share one. builder::set_arg0 leaks a buffer and carries the\n   fact. It moves when the options table moves, per error.rs.\n - Error has only two variants (Interrupted, Other). The sketch's Io and\n   the other eight are not promoted, so new failure paths use\n   Error::other(line, status, msg) with status 2 (sh_error's).\n - Host methods take no Shell ON PURPOSE — it keeps the host a leaf so\n   sh.host.set_signal() is a field-disjoint borrow, and makes re-entering\n   the shell from a callback a compile error."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T21:08:09.816793904Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "RESUMPTION NOTE — stretch 4 (supersedes stretch 3's item 1)\n\nLanded: cc5c7b9  fix: The host takes the SignalSink that already existed.\nGates at cc5c7b9: test 242/0, warnings 69, api-sketch+examples clean,\ndifferential PASS=61768 FAIL=0 STALE=0.\n\nTHE IMPORTANT FINDING, and a correction to my own work. `siginbox.rs`\nALREADY IS §5.3's SignalSink. Commit 837ae94 added a second type of the\nsame name in host.rs with a per-shell Arc and an invented pending-signal\narray. Both were wrong and are now removed:\n - siginbox's module doc had already settled the design: dispositions are\n   per PROCESS and onsig gets only signo, so it cannot know which Shell a\n   signal was for. Process-wide storage is CORRECT here, not a\n   compromise; §6 lists the inbox beside the locale and getopt. Do not\n   \"fix\" it into per-shell state — that is a regression.\n - Pending signals already live in error.rs's `intpending` and dash's\n   `gotsig`. There must not be a third store. When SignalSink::raise is\n   written it feeds THOSE.\n`host::SignalSink` is now `type SignalSink = &'static siginbox::SignalSink`.\n\nSIGNAL ROUTING (step 1) IS SMALLER THAN THE NOTE SAID. The \"nine sites\"\ndo not each call libc. There are exactly three libc chokepoints:\n  trap.rs:272  sigaction(signo, NULL, &act)   -- READ  -> Host::signal\n  trap.rs:311  sigaction(signo, &act, NULL)   -- WRITE -> Host::set_signal\n  trap.rs:325  signal(signo, SIG_IGN)         -- in ignoresig\nEverything else reaches signals THROUGH setsignal/ignoresig:\n  parent: builtins/trap.rs:111, trap.rs:149 (SIGCHLD), trap.rs:188\n          (clear_traps), trap.rs:491-493 (setinteractive),\n          jobs.rs:489-491 (setjobctl)\n  child:  jobs.rs:1036-1040 + 1061-1063 (forkchild, 7),\n          redir.rs:476-480 (here-doc, 5) = the twelve that STAY\nTHE HARD PART: setsignal/ignoresig are called from BOTH paths. A child\nmust get the disposition actually installed (it is a terminus and the\nprocess is wholly the shell's), so the child path must go DIRECT to libc,\nnot through the host — under NoHost a routed child call would silently do\nnothing and background jobs would stop ignoring SIGINT. So the split is\ntwo entry points (e.g. setsignal / setsignal_in_child), not a flag read\nfrom shell state.\nAlso note setsignal's S_HARD_IGN logic is exactly why Host::signal exists:\nit must READ the inherited disposition, and `act.sa_sigaction == SIG_IGN`\nbecomes `Disposition::Ignore`.\nnsh-cli needs a ProcessHost doing what dash does today (sigfillset on\nsa_mask, sa_flags = 0) and it MUST be passed in, or the frontend gets\nNoHost and every signal behaviour changes at once. ptydiff is the gate\nthat catches this (^C, ^Z, job control), not the corpus.\n\nRemaining, in order: (1) the routing above; (2) Source + Shell::run\nwithout EV_EXIT — the biggest piece left; (3) api.rs dedup of\nBuilder/Host/Disposition/SignalSink, BLOCKED ON (2) because embed.rs\ncalls sh.run()/sh.set_var() right after build() — twice proven, do not\nretry early; (4) embed.rs compiling AND running + remove the api-sketch\nfeature from Cargo.toml; (5) surface closure (only shellmain and streams\nhave external callers). Then the completion assessment over the\ntransferred obligations.\n\nSTANDING GATES for every slice from here: cargo test -p nsh; warnings\ncount; cargo check -p nsh --features api-sketch --examples; the full\ndifferential; and ptydiff for anything touching signals, job control or\nthe exit path."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:27:39.146422028Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "CLOSE-OUT — stretch 5, and the completion assessment. Six commits, `50f53c9`..`c9c66d2`, all landed on master by fast-forward, tree clean.\n\n  50f53c9  feat: The host installs dispositions; the forked child still does not\n  bc7156c  feat: Source and Shell::run, which is eval at the top level\n  d600ebb  feat: The rest of the embedder's surface: words, variables, capture\n  0e08c73  refactor: The sketch is gone, and the example runs against the real thing\n  21dc570  refactor: Thirty-eight public modules become two\n  c9c66d2  feat: Job control is a grant, and §11.5's open question is answered\n\n## The assessment: every transferred obligation, with evidence\n\n**From `move-state` (the streams/constructor entry).** Discharged at `ecfd861` by escape (2). The entry offered two ways to settle its debt — \"say so on `move-state`'s log, *or* move the field here directly\" — and the second is what happened, so nothing is owed to a node that is now sealed.\n\n**From `move-state` 1 of 2 (`io` rides with `streams`).** Discharged at `ecfd861`. Its two sub-items: the `out: *mut Output` soundness question became `output::Dest` at `081a106` (zero `*mut Output` parameters outside `output.rs`); `sh_warnx`'s entanglement was answered with the spine, as the entry asked.\n\n**From `move-state` 2 of 2 (the diagnostic sink).** Discharged at `c9c2434`, *against* the recommendation it carried, with the argument in the commit. `arg0` deliberately did not move — it is `$0` before it is a prefix. The six §5/§6 corrections applied at `deb7623`.\n\n**From `host-owns-signals` (the disposition half).** Discharged across `837ae94`, `cc5c7b9` and `50f53c9`. Its three inherited items:\n1. *Give the sink ownership (`Arc`, `attach`, a `Shell` field).* Answered differently and the difference is the finding: `siginbox` already **was** §5.3's sink, the `Arc` buys shareability the storage does not need, and `host::SignalSink` is `&'static siginbox::SignalSink`. `attach` exists and the host holds it.\n2. *§6 lists the inbox; do not design toward per-shell dispositions.* Honoured — `stretch 4`'s correction removed the per-shell copy that had been added by mistake.\n3. *`suppressint` should stop being counted against the signal group.* **Explicitly re-routed**, with a note on `errors-are-values` recording what is true about it now. This node moved no statics (39 at both ends), and `suppressint` is on no surface.\n\n**From `process-model`.** Resolutions 1–4 taken as settled and each honoured: `run` passes no `EV_EXIT` (`bc7156c`, and the `Shell::run` doc carries the argument); the forked-child constraint stands; the twelve child-side sites are not routed (`50f53c9`) — and the seam is *smaller* than resolution 3 said, because `clear_traps` turns out to be child-side at every reachable call site, derived in the commit; §7's `dup2` observation is untouched.\n* **A. Job control as a grant.** `c9c66d2`. A `Host` method rather than a builder flag, answering §11.5's open question in the document.\n* **B. `exitshell` returns.** `1b5440f`, and `run` now calls `exitreset` then `exitshell` in dash's order — which is how `exit 3` keeps its 3.\n* **C. Child-process pool and fork precondition in the crate docs.** `c9c66d2`, in `lib.rs`, which is now a surface a reader finds.\n* **D. `Shell::drop` neither waits nor kills, written down.** `c9c66d2`, same place.\n\n**The predecessor's own list (steps 1–9).** All done. The `api.rs` dedup was twice-deferred and correctly so; it landed at `0e08c73` once `run` and `set_var` existed. `examples/embed.rs` compiles **and runs**. The `api-sketch` feature is out of `Cargo.toml`.\n\n## Surface: 38 → 2\n\n`shellmain` and `streams`, which are the only two with external callers, exactly as the survey measured. Everything an embedder writes is re-exported at the crate root. **`#![deny(missing_docs)]` is on**, which is the property `[dec:nsh:public-surface]` asked to be measured under: it cost sixteen items, every one of which already had its comment in `/* */` form.\n\n## Two things found by running rather than reading\n\n* **`Streams::capture` did not hold what its docs claimed.** The sketch's sentence about the descriptors being \"the base of the shell's descriptor table\" was inherited into both `capture` and `from_fds`; a forked external command inherits the *process's* fd 1, and the logical-to-real table is §10's largest bet and is not built. `wc -l` wrote straight past the capture. Both docs corrected at `0e08c73`. **This is the one place the design document still overpromises, and §10 is where it is owed.**\n* **A `NoHost` shell's `wait` builtin can suspend forever.** Routing `SIGCHLD` through the host means a host that declines gets no handler, and `waitproc`'s `DOWAIT_WAITCMD` arm `sigsuspend`s on a signal that will not arrive. Foreground commands are fine — they block in `wait3`, which needs no handler. Recorded on the `Host` trait, sharper than `process-model` posed it.\n\n## Trend\n\n`static mut` declarations **39 → 39**, measured at `5a35b90` and at `c9c66d2` with one command in one session. Unchanged, and that is right: this node moved no statics.\n\n`unsafe fn` **493 → 501**. It rose. The honest reading is that the boundary moved rather than the total — the eight are private helpers that touch libc or the input stack, and **every public entry point this node added is a safe fn**: `run`, `run_command`, `expand_word`, `expand_word_quoted`, `var`, `set_var`, `unset_var`, `vars`, `status`, `has_exited`, `take_captured_stdout`/`_stderr`, all of `Builder`, all of `Streams`. Note the briefed baseline of 487 was measured at `ab132ba` with an unrecorded command; 493 is what this session's command gives at this node's base, so the delta is the figure to trust and the absolute is not comparable to the earlier one.\n\n## Gates at `c9c66d2`\n\n`cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean. `cargo test -p nsh` **258/0** (242 at the node's base, plus 16). Warnings **69** at every commit, with the *set* diffed against master and byte-identical each time. `cargo run -p nsh --example embed` runs to completion — this replaces the `api-sketch` example gate, which is gone with the feature. Full differential **PASS=61768 FAIL=0 XFAIL=4 STALE=0**, run four times across the stretch. ptydiff **PASS=60 FAIL=0**, run four times — it is the gate that mattered twice here, for the signal routing and for the job-control grant, because the corpus has no controlling terminal and delivers no signal.\n\nPOSIX not run: nothing here changes what the shell accepts or how it answers. The differential is the conformance oracle for that and it is unchanged.\n\n## What is left, and is not this node's\n\n* **§10's per-instance descriptor table.** The largest bet in the document, unbuilt, and now with a concrete cost attached: `Streams::capture` cannot hold a forked command's output and `streams::set` cannot make redirection agree with the shell's own streams. `streams::install` remains the full-fidelity mode and is process-wide.\n* **`$0` is still a process-global** (`options::arg0`), so two shells in one process share one. `builder::set_arg0` leaks a buffer and carries the fact. It moves when the options table moves.\n* **The signal inbox and its trapped mirror are process-wide**, correctly (§6), so a second `Shell` resets the first one's trap bits. `TrapTable::new`'s comment carries this.\n* **`suppressint`**, re-routed to `errors-are-values` above."
                    }

