                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T10:14:23.788378187Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "Found while sweeping libc calls out of the crate for [[delete-libc-calls]], in a review of that node's diff that widened to its neighbours. These are in `bbfb5dd`/`5a259a4`, not in the sweep, and none of them is caught by the corpora: every one needs an argument the generators do not produce.\n\nTwo are reproduced here against `tests/.build/ref/src/dash`:\n\n* **`printf '%.65536f' 1` panics the shell.** `conv.rs:236` (and 245, 262, 279) renders through `format!(\\\"{magnitude:.precision$}\\\")`, and Rust's formatter holds precision in a `u16`, so a precision above 65535 is \\\"Formatting argument out of range\\\" and exit 101. The C hands the number to glibc and prints 65538 bytes at exit 0. A builtin that aborts the process is worse than one that prints the wrong thing, and `[dec:nsh:shell-as-library]` makes it worse again: this kills the host, not a subshell.\n* **`\\v` stopped being a leading blank.** `printf.rs:183` uses `u8::is_ascii_whitespace`, which excludes 0x0B; C's `isspace` includes it. `printf '%d\\\\n' \\\"$(printf '\\\\v42')\\\"` is `42` and exit 0 in the C, and \\\"expected numeric value\\\" plus `0` and exit 1 here. It reaches every numeric conversion and every `*` width or precision.\n\nReported unverified, from the same review, and each worth confirming before acting on:\n\n* Hex float literals in the subnormal range collapse to zero — `printf.rs:421` computes `magnitude * exp2(exponent)` and `exp2` flushes below -1074, so `0x1.fffffffffffffp-1023` prints `0` rather than `2.22507e-308`.\n* Hex float literals never report ERANGE — `printf.rs:317` hardcodes the flag false on that path, so `printf '%f\\\\n' 0x1p9999` prints `inf` at exit 0 where the C warns and exits 1.\n* A width or precision at or above 2^31 turns a diagnostic into a 2 GiB emission — the C answered `printf: xvsnprintf failed` with no output and exit 2.\n* `%#g` differs at a rounding carry: `printf '%#g\\\\n' 999999.5` was `1.e+06` and is now `1.00000e+06`. The new output is what C99 describes and the old is glibc's quirk, so this one may want a divergence entry rather than a fix.\n* `*` after digits in a width (`%5*d`) is undefined and the two disagree on which nonsense they emit.\n\nNot actioned under [[delete-libc-calls]]: that node is a pure refactor whose oracle is \\\"nothing changes\\\", and fixing a printf defect inside it would spend that guarantee. The first item is the one that wants a node of its own."
                        after_done true
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T11:24:55.954257107Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "Found under [[printf-errata]] while establishing the oracle for the hexadecimal range report, and not one of the seven that node was opened for. `scan_double`'s *decimal* path reports ERANGE on the wrong rule, at exactly the boundary the hexadecimal path was corrected at.\n\n`printf.rs` asks `nonzero && value.abs() < f64::MIN_POSITIVE`: tininess measured after the value has already been rounded into the destination. C reports IEEE underflow, which is tininess after rounding to 53 bits with *no bound on the exponent*, together with a result that could not be held exactly. The two differ in both directions, and both reproduce against `tests/.build/ref/src/dash` with the value agreeing and only the status and the warning apart:\n\n* `printf '%a' 2.2250738585072012e-308` is `0x1p-1022` in both. The C warns `Numerical result out of range` and exits 1, because rounding the literal to 53 bits leaves it below the smallest normal. The port is silent at exit 0, because the double it landed on is not below it.\n* The exact decimal expansion of 2^-1074, all 751 significant digits of it, is `0x0.0000000000001p-1022` in both. The C is silent because the literal is held exactly. The port warns, because the double is subnormal and the literal was nonzero.\n\nNot fixed under [[printf-errata]], and deliberately so. The hexadecimal path could be corrected because it lays the bits out itself and therefore knows what it dropped; the decimal path hands the digits to `str::parse`, which returns the double and nothing at all about the rounding that produced it. Matching C means scanning a decimal literal into the same mantissa-and-sticky form `round_to_double` already takes -- a decimal-to-binary conversion the port does not have, and a good deal larger than anything in that node's brief. It wants its own node, and a divergence-register entry in the meantime if it is to be left standing.\n\nNo corpus reaches it. None of the three fuzz generators produces a literal within a rounding of the subnormal boundary, which is why 12000 cases across six sweeps have never seen it."
                        after_done true
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T12:25:14.879005321Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "Second finding from the review under [[printf-errata]], and like the decimal-ERANGE entry above it, not one of the seven and not fixed there. At a field width of exactly `INT_MAX` the *reference* loses the last byte of the conversion, and the port does not.\n\n    $ dash -c 'printf \"%*d\" 2147483647 1' | tr -d \" \" | od -c   ->  \\0\n    $ nsh  -c 'printf \"%*d\" 2147483647 1' | tr -d \" \" | od -c   ->   1\n\nBoth write 2147483647 bytes, so the length has always agreed and every check that counted bytes has passed. The content does not: dash's `xvasprintf` asks `stalloc` for `len + 1` and that addition overflows an `int` at exactly `INT_MAX`, so glibc is handed a size one short and truncates the last character to the terminating NUL. `%2147483647.0f` does the same. One below the limit, and at any smaller width, the two are byte-identical.\n\nThis is a defect in the oracle rather than in the port, and reproducing it means reproducing an integer overflow inside dash's own allocator. It is also expensive to pin: a case costs two gigabytes in each shell, which is why the corpus stops at `%.2147483647a` and at widths of 65536.\n\nLeft standing deliberately. If it is to be kept, it wants a divergence-register entry saying the port declines the C's overflow, which is a decision rather than a defect and belongs to whoever owns that register -- [[printf-errata]] was told to add no entries to it."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id printf-errata
                title "Fix the printf errata: the %.65536f shell panic and the review's six other reports"
                kind @fix
                deps (printf-builtin)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T10:17:50.098828971Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T11:24:17.944005611Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Probe of the note path."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T11:24:43.091429483Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Correcting the entry above: \"Probe of the note path.\" records nothing. It was written to find out why an `errata` entry on this node was refused while a `note` was accepted, which is that errata attaches to work already done. The finding it was clearing the way for is filed where it belongs, as errata on [[printf-builtin]].\n\nVerdict on the seven reports, each checked against `tests/.build/ref/src/dash` before being acted on. All seven were real; none was refuted.\n\n1. `%.65536f` panics the shell. Confirmed, and wider than reported: the same panic sat under `%e` and twice under `%g`, and `%.65536d`, `%65536d` and `%-65536s` were fine because none of them reaches `format!`'s precision. Fixed in `7326edd`.\n2. `\\v` is not a leading blank. Confirmed exactly as described. Fixed in `6c10b10`.\n3. Hexadecimal subnormals collapse to zero. Confirmed, though not by the example given: `%f` prints `0.000000` for the true value too, so the collapse only shows under `%e` or `%a`. Fixed in `7861166`.\n4. Hexadecimal literals never report ERANGE. Confirmed. The rule turned out to be narrower than \"subnormal\": C reports IEEE underflow, which is tininess *after* rounding together with an inexact result, so `0x1p-1074` is silent and `0x1.fffffffffffff8p-1023` is silent while `0x1.fffffffffffffp-1023` warns despite reaching the same double. Fixed in `7861166`.\n5. A width or precision at or above 2^31 emits 2 GiB where C diagnoses. Confirmed, with the boundary one place off from the report: `%2147483647d` emits its two gigabytes in *both* shells and is correct; the refusal starts one past it. The real rule is `vsnprintf`'s `int` return, so a rendered length past `INT_MAX` is refused too even when the precision that named it is in range. Fixed in `7326edd`.\n6. `%#g` differs at a rounding carry. Confirmed. The report's own suggestion was a divergence entry rather than a fix, on the grounds that the port's output is what C99 describes; this node's brief forbids new register entries and names dash the oracle, so it is a fix. Narrower than reported: it applies only when the carry is what moved the style from f to e, so `%#.3g 999999.5` was already right. Fixed in `6604452`.\n7. `%5*d` is undefined and the two disagree. Confirmed. glibc writes an unreadable specification back out verbatim, and what it writes is the text as the C passed it -- with `mklong`'s `l` inserted and `%b`'s conversion character set to `s`. Fixed in `30fe9ff`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T12:44:06.822539927Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id statics-that-are-not-state
                title "Retire the statics that are not shell state: two call-scoped cursors become parameters, four const tables become immutable"
                priority 0
                kind @refactor
                deps (no-ambient-state)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T15:03:06.971511109Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "**Created out of `move-state`'s close-out, carrying the part of its remainder that is unblocked but is *not* shell state. Six statics, in two unrelated groups. Nothing depends on this node, and it should not be allowed to look like it blocks anything.**\n\n`move-state` moved every table of shell state it could and completed; the rest of the crate's `static mut` declarations are blocked on other nodes' design answers, excluded by `docs/api-design.md` §5.1/§5.2/§5.3, or per-call working state that is correctly not a field. These six are the exception: unblocked, small, and wrong as statics for reasons that have nothing to do with the shell instance.\n\n**Group 1 — two call-scoped cursors that §5.2 says must not be fields, and should be parameters.** `move-state` found these while moving `parser.rs`'s eleven globals and deliberately left them, because making them fields would have satisfied a \"no `static mut`\" count while recording the opposite of what is true about them.\n\n* **`parser.rs checkkwd`** is a mode flag written immediately before a `readtoken` call and zeroed by it — an argument spelled as a global. It has three roles, catalogued in `74628e7`: an in-parameter to `readtoken`; a *two-level* parameter, where `parsefname` sets `CHKEOFMARK` and `readtoken1` reads it two frames down; and a side-channel from the input layer, which `74628e7` already removed (that was the one crossing a module boundary). What remains is roles one and two, both internal to `parser.rs`. `readtoken` has many callers, so the in-parameter conversion is the wider of the two.\n* **`parser.rs quoteflag`** is written exactly once, from `readtoken1`'s local `st.quotef`, purely to carry a value past a `return`. It belongs in that function's return type. This is the smaller and cleaner of the two and could go first.\n\n**Group 2 — four const tables that are `static mut` only because their element type is not `Sync`.** None is ever written. `move-state` did this to `options.rs`'s `optnames` in `4378d0f` and the recipe is there: `[*const c_char; N]` became `[&CStr; N]`, which *is* `Sync`, the mutability went, and the NUL every reader relied on moved out of a comment and into the type — deleting a `CStr::from_ptr` at each call site as a side effect.\n\n* `builtins/mod.rs bltin`\n* `builtins/test.rs ops`\n* `arith_yylex.rs arith_names`\n* `mystring.rs nullstr`\n\nCheck each for the same shape rather than assuming; `nullstr` in particular is read as a pointer in many places and may want a different spelling.\n\n**Ordering.** Dep'd on `no-ambient-state` so the frontier ranks it after that area rather than beside it — group 1 touches `parser.rs`, which `move-state` has just rewritten, and doing it first would mean conflicting edits for no gain. Beyond that there is no urgency: **this is tidying, and the honest framing is that it removes six declarations that were never the problem.** The `static mut` count is not the point and should not be used to argue for scheduling it — `docs/errors-are-values.md` §0.6 records why that count is a weak metric and how to take it properly.\n\n**Expected effect:** `static mut` declarations 49 → 43, unsafe ops roughly flat (these are reads of statics that become reads of consts; the count moves when an `addr_of_mut!` funnel disappears, and there is none here)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:49:16.435757201Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "**Transfer from [[owned-words]], with the measurement rather than an estimate: `expand.rs`'s remaining unsafe is now *mostly yours*, and the shape of it is four accessors.**\n\n`owned-words` finished converting word expansion to owned bytes at `f9f8ebe`. `expand.rs` went 490 → 242 unsafe operations over that node. Here is what the 242 are, by `cargo check --lib` with `allow(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)` removed, counting `warning[E0133]` headers paired with the `-->` beneath each:\n\n```\n 98  the `static mut` accessors, and INTOFF/INTON\n       expb()        32     the expansion buffer\n       ifsr()        24     ifsregions\n       expargl()     10     exparg.list\n       expdest_off()  8     the expansion buffer's cursor\n       direct `use of mutable static`  8\n       INTON/INTOFF  15     error.rs's suppressint, not expand.rs's\n 23  the libc/FFI floor — transferred to [[unsafe-is-a-crate]] separately\n 21  raw-pointer dereferences (see below)\n100  calls to functions that are still `unsafe fn` for the reasons above\n```\n\n**So 98 of 242 are directly yours, and most of the trailing 100 collapses behind them** — those are calls like `argstr`, `subevalvar`, `expandmeta`, `recordregion`, `strtodest`, which are `unsafe fn` *only* because their bodies still touch a static. They stop being `unsafe fn` when the statics go, and the call sites stop counting.\n\n**The 21 raw dereferences, every one located, because \"raw pointer\" now means something different here than it did.** None of them is a word-text walk any more:\n\n* `419`, `429`, `460` — the bodies of `ifsr()`, `expargl()`, `expb()`: `&mut *ptr::addr_of_mut!(…)`. Yours.\n* `838`, `1059`, `1356` — `&mut *addr_of_mut!(exparg)` in `expandarg`, and the two `argbackq` slice-cursor reads. Yours.\n* `2124`, `2164`, `2198`, `2220`, `2228` — `varvalue`: `*name` twice, `atoi(name)`, `*shellparam.p().offset(n-1)`, and two `*ap` walks of the positional-parameter vector. **Yours** — your own log already inventories `shellparam` at 40 sites. `owned-words` deliberately stopped at this boundary and left `varvalue` taking the variable's *name* as a `char *`, with a comment at its call site in `evalvar` saying so, because past it the work is the variable table's and not the word's.\n* `780` — `(*pw).pw_dir`, `getpwnam`'s result. [[unsafe-is-a-crate]].\n* `3216`, `3219`, `3223`–`3225` — `expmeta`'s `dirent64` fields from `readdir64`. [[unsafe-is-a-crate]].\n* `2798`, `2801`, `2803` — `expandmeta_glob`'s `gl_pathv`. **Dead**: `GLOB_IS_ENABLED` is a `const bool` that is `false` (`mystring.rs:74` → `expand.rs:98`), its sole call site is `if GLOB_IS_ENABLED { … }`, and `system.rs`'s `glob64` stub returns `-1` unconditionally with a test pinning that. Type-checked dead code, not `#[cfg]`, which is why it still counts.\n* `3035` — `memrchr`, the C's `#ifndef HAVE_MEMRCHR` fallback, kept because the `#ifndef` is in the C. Its caller became `rfind_byte`.\n\n**One thing worth knowing before you start on `expbuf`.** Its cursor is already gone: `expdest`, `expbase`, `set_expdest` and `expmakestrspace` were retired at `1e8047c` when `_rmescapes` stopped holding a pointer into it. What is left is `expb()` itself and `expdest_off()`, which is just `expbuf.len()`. So the buffer is a plain `BString` behind a `static mut` and nothing addresses it by pointer — the conversion you face is the ownership question alone, with no aliasing left underneath it."
                    }

