            {
                id unsafe-is-a-crate
                title "The necessary unsafe lives in one crate behind safe wrappers; nsh denies unsafe_code"
                priority 0
                kind @refactor
                deps (
                    process-model
                    owned-words
                    host-owns-signals
                    statics-that-are-not-state
                )
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:49:39.677727033Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "**Transfer from [[owned-words]]: the syscall/FFI floor `expand.rs` still stands on, itemised, plus a measured update to this node's own baseline.**\n\n`owned-words` finished at `f9f8ebe`. Of `expand.rs`'s remaining 242 unsafe operations, **23 are the floor this node is about**, and they are not spread thin — they are three clusters:\n\n* **`expmeta`'s directory walk (16).** `libc::opendir`, `readdir64`, `closedir`, `lstat64`, the `ptr::read_volatile`/`write_volatile` pair the C uses for its `DIR *volatile`, and five dereferences of `dirent64` fields (`(*dp).d_name`, `d_type` ×3, and the first byte of the entry name at `3216`/`3219`/`3223`–`3225`). `expmeta` went 60 → 20 operations under `owned-words`, and what stopped it there is exactly this: the function's remaining unsafety *is* the directory API. Its `name` parameter is a `&[u8]` and its candidate buffer is an index-addressed `BString`; nothing else in it is a pointer.\n* **`getpwhome` (2).** `libc::getpwnam` and `(*pw).pw_dir` at `780`. The one caller, `exptilde`, now hands it a NUL-terminated copy it owns rather than terminating the word in place.\n* **The multibyte decoders (5).** `mbrlen` in `mbtodest`, `mbrtowc` in `ifsisifs` and `ccmatch_bytes`, `mem::zeroed` for the `mbstate_t` each of them starts from.\n\n**Two of those are already safe wrappers, and they are worth copying rather than re-deriving.** `mbtodest` and `pmatch_slices` are *safe* functions with the unsafe operation in a small block inside, and the argument that makes them safe is the same one both times: `mbrlen`/`fnmatch` want a length, that length used to be a number the caller had to get right, and it is now the slice's own `len()`. That is the shape this node is trying to reach — the obligation discharged at the boundary rather than propagated — and `expand.rs` has two worked examples of it.\n\n**A correction to this node's recorded baseline, because it was measured before three nodes ran.** The decision text on `[[minimal-unsafe]]` anatomises 3271 operations at `17fc5c6` and says the deref class is \"concentrated in expand.rs (639 total ops) and parser.rs (313)\". Re-measured at `f9f8ebe` by the same method (`docs/errors-are-values.md` §0.6): **the crate is at 2779, expand.rs at 242, parser.rs at 269.** So expand.rs is no longer the largest concentration in the crate and has not been since `3e0d763`; `parser.rs` is, and per the survey recorded on `[[owned-words]]` its residue is `checkkwd`/`quoteflag` statics and the input layer rather than C strings. Crate-wide, raw-pointer dereferences are **543 → 472** and `unsafe fn` declarations **507 → 491** across `owned-words`' seven slices.\n\nThe endpoint this node sets — the floor and nothing else — is now a much shorter distance in `expand.rs` than the decision's figures suggest, and the 23 above are the whole of it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:50:39.766405198Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "**`process-model` closing: the floor is enumerated, and the operator directive's six-word summary of it is 170 of 255 sites.** This is the node's main output for you, and it resolves `[dec:nsh:minimal-unsafe]`'s deferred consequence (\"where the floor actually sits… never been counted separately from the incidental unsafety around them\"), which is applied in `plan/decisions/minimal-unsafe.md` at `ccca396`. `docs/api-design.md` §11.3 carries the table.\n\n## The measurement\n\nCommand, run over `crates/nsh/src` at `410e729` (in-file `#[cfg(test)]` modules included):\n\n```\ngrep -rhoE 'libc::[a-z_0-9]+\\(' src/ --include=*.rs | sed 's/libc:://;s/(//' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\n```\n\n**255 `libc::` call sites across 68 distinct symbols**, plus **13 symbols hand-declared in 7 `extern \"C\"` blocks** because `libc` 0.2 does not bind them. **One vendor**: there is no `nix` and no direct `rustix` in `crates/nsh`. `rustix` is already linked transitively under `nshedit-plat`, so adding it directly costs nothing in tree size — the cost is 255 call sites and 7 blocks, not a dependency.\n\n| Group | Symbols | `libc::` sites |\n|---|---|---|\n| Process creation / exec | `fork` `vfork` `execve` `_exit` | 26 |\n| Wait / reaping | `waitpid` `sigsuspend` — and `wait3`† | 2 |\n| Process groups / terminal | `setpgid` `getpgrp` `tcgetpgrp` `tcsetpgrp` `killpg` `tcgetattr` `isatty` | 14 |\n| Signals | `sigaction` `signal` `sigprocmask` `sigfillset` `sigemptyset` `sigaddset` `sigismember` `kill` `raise` `strsignal` | 34 |\n| Descriptors | `close` `open64` `dup` `dup2` `pipe` `fcntl` `lseek` `read` `write` `memfd_create` `tee` `mkstemp` `unlink` `fpathconf` | 94 |\n| stat family | `stat64` `lstat64` `fstat64` `faccessat` | 20 |\n| Directory iteration | `opendir` `readdir64` `closedir` | 4 |\n| Identity | `getpid` `getppid` `geteuid` `getegid` `getgroups` `getpwnam` | 14 |\n| Limits / times / umask | `umask` `getrlimit` `setrlimit` `times` `sysconf` | 13 |\n| Environment / locale | `setlocale` `getenv` `putenv` — and `environ`† | 5 |\n| errno | `__errno_location` | 3 |\n| `getopt` | `getopt` — and `optind`† `optopt`† `optarg`† | 1 |\n| C library, not syscalls | `strerror` `strcoll` `fnmatch` `atoi` `isalpha` `isalnum` `isspace` `isdigit` — and `strtoimax`† `mbrlen`† `mbrtowc`† `mbsrtowcs`† `iswspace`† `wctype`† `iswctype`† `iswblank`† | 25 |\n\n† hand-declared, so not counted in the column. The column sums to exactly 255, which is the check that the grouping lost nothing.\n\nThe seven non-coverage `extern \"C\"` blocks: `jobs.rs:1452` (`wait3`), `system.rs:114` and `arith_yylex.rs:245` (`__isoc23_strtoimax`, via `#[link_name]` — binding the plain symbol silently loses `0b` literals, so `$((0b11))` would stop being 3), `expand.rs:46` (`mbrlen` `mbrtowc` `mbsrtowcs` `iswspace` `wctype` `iswctype`), `parser.rs:2554` (`mbrtowc` `iswblank`), `var.rs:33` (`environ`), `builtins/fc.rs:29` (`optind` `optopt` `optarg`). Two more in `shell.rs` are `#[cfg(coverage)]`-only (`__llvm_profile_*`).\n\n## Three findings, in the order they will cost you\n\n**1. The floor is not the syscalls, and the plan log's endpoint sentence should be read with that in mind.** The directive names it as \"fork/exec/wait/signals/termios/fd ops\". **That is 170 of the 255.** The other 85 are `stat`, identity, resource limits, `errno`, `getopt` and the C library's string, ctype, collation and multibyte routines. Not syscalls — still FFI, and **every one has to move before `nsh` can deny `unsafe_code`**, which is the actual endpoint.\n\nThe last group is the hard one and it is hard for a reason wrapping does not address. `strcoll`, `isalpha`, `isspace`, `mbrtowc` and `fnmatch` are **locale-dependent**, so replacing them is a behaviour decision, not a safety one — and `docs/api-design.md` §6's first entry (the locale is process-wide, one `Shell` assigning `LC_COLLATE` changes how another sorts a glob) is exactly why. `mystring.rs:132-139` already records that `isspace` keeping `\\v` in the space class is observable from shell (`exit $'1\\v'`). **Wrap that group; do not reimplement it**, or at least do not reimplement it under this node.\n\n**2. About half the floor is already wrapped, and the unwrapped half is not a random half.**\n\n*Wrapped, `Result`-returning:* `redir::{sh_open, sh_pipe, sh_dup2, savefd}`, `jobs::{forkshell, vforkexec, xtcsetpgrp, xxtcsetpgrp, waitproc}`, `trap::{setsignal, ignoresig, sigblockall}`, `system::{sigclearmask, errno}`, `output::{write_fd_once, write_fd, xwrite}`, `streams::{install, Borrowed::restore}`, `siginbox::SignalsBlocked`.\n\n*Raw at 90-odd sites, no wrapper at all:* `setpgid`, `getpgrp`, `tcgetpgrp`, `isatty`, `close`, `stat64`, `kill`, `killpg`, `raise`, `_exit`.\n\n**That unwrapped list is almost exactly `[dec:nsh:host-owns-the-process]`'s list of grant-bearing operations**, and it is not a coincidence: the calls nobody had to think about are the calls nobody wrapped. Expect the wrapping pass and the grant pass to touch the same lines, and sequence them so they do not collide.\n\n*One live inconsistency to fix while you are there:* `eval.rs:859` calls `libc::pipe` directly while `redir.rs:407` routes the same syscall through `redir::sh_pipe`, and both then build the same `\"Pipe call failed\"` diagnostic in two places (`eval.rs:866`, `redir.rs:408`).\n\n**3. A hard constraint on the crate's error type, from `[dec:nsh:fork-child-is-a-terminus]`.** The wrappers a forked child calls between `fork`/`vfork` and `execve` — `execve`, `_exit`, `dup2`, `close`, `open`, `setpgid`, `tcsetpgrp`, `signal`, `sigaction` — **must not allocate**. Between `vfork` and `execve` the child runs in the parent's address space; between `fork` and `execve` in a multithreaded host it may run with the allocator lock held by a thread that no longer exists.\n\nSo the error type is a bare `errno`, never `Box<dyn Error>`, never `String`, never anything that renders a message on construction. `output-is-a-writer` and `[dec:nsh:printf-is-parsed-not-interpreted]` arrived at the same shape for unrelated reasons — the value carries the facts, the call site does the rendering — so three decisions now want it. **Cheap in the first commit, expensive to retrofit.**\n\n## Two notes on measuring your own trend\n\n`siginbox.rs` is the worked example of retiring a `static mut` without losing async-signal-safety: `static SINK: SignalSink` of atomics, reached through `signals()`, so what it absorbed left the count rather than moving within it. `output.rs` and `streams.rs` are the worked examples of the other shape — the only two modules where explicit `unsafe {` blocks outnumber `unsafe fn` declarations (29 vs 15, and 16 vs 3), i.e. safe functions with narrow unsafe interiors. Everywhere else the ratio is inverted.\n\nAnd keep `[dec:nsh:we-own-the-defects]`'s discipline about figures: this node's own `unsafe fn` correction on `owned-words` records that a delta needs the same command run at both ends, in the same hour. Every number above is one command over one commit (`410e729`); the command is written out so it can be re-run rather than trusted."
                    }

